WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:10.460
So today, we're going to
finish this whirlwind tour

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:12.890
through problems.

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:15.590
Different types of problems
and formulations and things

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:18.650
like that, and some
named problem classes.

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:21.920
So last time I think we
quit right around here

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:23.940
on quasiconvex optimization.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:29.660
So that's when you minimize
a quasiconvex objective.

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:33.050
And here, you have to be--

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:36.300
I mean, you should be a little
bit worried because, in fact,

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:37.640
the basic--

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:40.677
probably the most important
fact about convex problems

00:00:40.677 --> 00:00:42.260
is that you can have
a local minimum--

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:45.560
you can't have a local minimum
that's not a global minimum.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:48.440
But for quasiconvex
optimization, that's false.

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:50.330
You can have that.

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:52.790
So you have to be a
little bit more careful.

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:56.040
Not that much careful, it's
not that hard to solve.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:01.760
So the way you do this is if
a function is quasiconvex,

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:03.440
it means its sublevel
sets are convex.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.560
And so what we
introduce is a function

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:12.120
whose zero sublevel set
is the key sublevel set

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:14.530
of your function.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:19.320
And so this function
has to be convex.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.870
So if-- and how you
construct phi t,

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:24.960
there is completely generic
constructions of it.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:28.020
They're not useful, but
when people ask you,

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:31.530
how do you come up with that
phi t, you can always do it.

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:34.020
Often it just is
suggested by the problem.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.693
We looked at an example
last time that was this.

00:01:36.693 --> 00:01:38.110
I mean, here's an
interesting one.

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:44.640
It's the ratio of a non-negative
over a positive function.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:48.750
The numerator is convex and
the denominator is concave.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:54.090
So this function here, it
is definitely not convex,

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:57.660
necessarily, but
it is quasiconvex.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:01:59.640
And to check that,
what you do is you look

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:01.130
at the sublevel set of this.

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:03.720
A p-- when is it true that
p of x divided by q of x

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:06.140
is less than or equal to t?

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:11.060
If t is negative,
then this is simple

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:14.420
because then the sublevel set
is empty and that's convex,

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:15.980
so that's cool,
so we really only

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:17.640
have to worry about t positive.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:22.970
So if t is positive, then p of
x over q of x is less than t.

00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:25.650
Well, this is true
without the t positive.

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:30.410
That's true if and only if p
of x minus tq of x is less than

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:32.210
or equal to 0.

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:33.830
Now the critical part.

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:39.170
This is a convex function of
x, provided t is positive.

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:41.810
That's because q is concave.

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:44.690
So you're just using one
of these simple rules

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:45.380
in your head.

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:49.040
That's concave, multiplied by t
positive, still concave, minus,

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:51.740
now it's convex,
and then it's a sum.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:54.960
So this is the example.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.670
And what this allows you
to do is really cool.

00:02:57.670 --> 00:02:59.700
It says that if I
wanted to check,

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:03.720
does there exist an x for
which f0 of x is less than t?

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:07.500
I can check it by solving a
convex feasibility problem.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:11.197
So that's the idea here,
which is really cool.

00:03:11.197 --> 00:03:12.780
And then, of course,
what you could do

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:14.280
is you can just do bisection.

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:18.060
You just say, well, can
you find an x where f0 of x

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:19.350
is less than 1.5?

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:21.705
And if the answer is
yes, then you go cool.

00:03:21.705 --> 00:03:23.580
And by the way, you now
have a feasible point

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:26.640
with an objective
value of 1.5 or better.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.760
Then you'd say, all right,
how about 1 or how about 0.5

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:30.810
or how about minus 1?

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:31.870
This kind of thing.

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:35.190
So-- I mean, not minus 1
in this case, but still.

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:37.560
So anyway, that's
just bisection,

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:39.540
and that actually works.

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:45.040
So the algorithm is
actually just what I said.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:49.060
I start with an upper and a
lower bound on the optimal

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:51.250
value of the objective.

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:52.870
I just query--

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:57.370
I ask-- what I do is I take the
midpoint of that interval and I

00:03:57.370 --> 00:04:00.220
ask, is there a is there a--

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:02.740
I solve a convex
feasibility problem

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:05.740
to find out if these
inequalities hold.

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:08.650
If they do, then that tells
you that p star is less than

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:10.720
or equal to that value.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:12.910
It means you're
above-- and by the way,

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:15.040
now I update my upper
bound, I pull it

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:17.140
down to this halfway mark.

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:19.600
If it goes the other way
around, I take my lower bound

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:21.279
and I pull it up to
the halfway mark.

00:04:21.279 --> 00:04:23.800
And what this tells
you is that your--

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:27.970
let's see, your interval of
ignorance about the optimal

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:31.000
value, that's-- p star
is in some interval,

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:35.843
that interval shrinks by a
factor of 2 every iteration.

00:04:35.843 --> 00:04:37.510
Or if you like, you
could even say this.

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:41.840
You could say that you
obtain one bit of information

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:45.410
in about p star
every time you solve

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:47.360
one of these convex
feasibility problems.

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:51.560
And by one bit, I mean that
it's-- you actually reduce

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:54.710
your uncertainty exactly
by a factor of 2.

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:56.480
So that's it.

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:59.300
And this is standard
and it works and so on.

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:03.970
So that's quasiconvex
optimization.

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:04.600
OK.

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:05.650
What we're going
to do now is we're

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:07.983
going to talk about a whole
bunch of different problems.

00:05:07.983 --> 00:05:09.470
Some of them
actually have names.

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:12.580
So there's named
problem classes.

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:15.530
I'm just trying to think,
it's maybe much less important

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:19.240
these days that these, but
things like this is just basic,

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:21.640
and there'll be people who
don't know other stuff,

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:25.660
and they'll know these-- so you
just have to know these names.

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:28.720
So linear program
is a famous one.

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:30.970
That's LP.

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:33.940
And we already encountered
this a couple of weeks ago.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:38.170
It's minimize an affine
function subject to a set

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:42.220
of affine inequality
constraints here and an equality

00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:42.790
constraint.

00:05:42.790 --> 00:05:45.910
Or another way to say it is,
the d doesn't matter here.

00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:49.090
If I remove d, this becomes
an equivalent problem.

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:51.185
It's not the same
it's equivalent.

00:05:51.185 --> 00:05:52.310
It's an equivalent problem.

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:54.730
And so basically it says
minimize the linear function

00:05:54.730 --> 00:05:55.990
over a polyhedron.

00:05:55.990 --> 00:05:58.240
So that's actually
another way to state

00:05:58.240 --> 00:05:59.890
what a linear program is.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:06.860
So actually, this-- the
modern history starts

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:09.300
in around the '40s for
linear programming,

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:11.240
but in fact, I
think Fourier wrote

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:13.080
a paper about
linear programming,

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:18.440
so that tells you
it's not totally new.

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:20.600
It became a whole
lot more interesting

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:22.580
when you could
actually compute stuff.

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:24.890
So that would be after
the age of computers.

00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:26.690
So OK.

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:28.400
So picture looks like this.

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:29.450
Here's your feasible set.

00:06:29.450 --> 00:06:30.560
It's a polyhedron.

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:35.720
Each of these faces is a row,
a single linear inequality.

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:36.758
So that's what this is.

00:06:36.758 --> 00:06:38.300
And so these are
the feasible points.

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:43.220
And there's a vector c,
it points this direction.

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:46.650
These dashed lines show you the
level curves of the objective,

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:48.410
which is c transpose x.

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:50.990
Obviously it's going to
be orthogonal-- they're

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:52.820
orthogonal to c.

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:59.090
And what it says is this
might be 3, 2, 1, 0.

00:06:59.090 --> 00:06:59.840
Like-- and so on.

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:02.840
I mean, this would be minus
1, minus 2, and so on.

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:06.770
And so your job is to
basically slide in this thing,

00:07:06.770 --> 00:07:09.900
go this direction as
far as you possibly can,

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:13.280
and that would be
like this point here.

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:14.900
I mean, this is very--

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:17.660
I mean, this picture is not a
bad one to have in your mind

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:20.300
because it explains
what an what an LP is.

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:23.450
Obviously we are not
interested in solving

00:07:23.450 --> 00:07:25.130
LPs with two variables
because you could

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:26.840
do that with your eyeball.

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:30.320
So what we're
interested in is solving

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:36.110
LPs with 10,000 variables
and 30,000 constraints.

00:07:36.110 --> 00:07:38.390
People do that all--
like right now,

00:07:38.390 --> 00:07:40.910
I guarantee you, a lot of
those are being done right now.

00:07:40.910 --> 00:07:42.327
It's used for all
sorts of things,

00:07:42.327 --> 00:07:44.870
like scheduling
supply chain stuff.

00:07:44.870 --> 00:07:46.446
It's just used everywhere.

00:07:46.446 --> 00:07:50.270
And so, in my opinion,
everyone should know about it.

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:52.630
This is linear programming.

00:07:52.630 --> 00:07:55.950
So actually, the first
example is historical,

00:07:55.950 --> 00:07:59.010
and I think that's it's
cool because it's--

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:01.620
well, it's just
respecting history anyway.

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:02.670
So this is from--

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:05.970
maybe from maybe 1940s or '50s.

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:08.380
And it's the diet problem.

00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:11.670
So I think it
allegedly was supposed

00:08:11.670 --> 00:08:13.980
to be from the military.

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:17.160
Like how do you
construct a diet that

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:20.730
will keep soldiers alive
and as cheap as possible

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:21.960
or something like that?

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:22.890
I mean, whatever.

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:24.930
So it's a perfectly
good problem.

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:26.250
Here it is.

00:08:26.250 --> 00:08:28.728
So you're going to
have a bunch of foods

00:08:28.728 --> 00:08:30.520
and you're going to
choose some quantities.

00:08:30.520 --> 00:08:32.909
These are going to
be non-negative.

00:08:32.909 --> 00:08:35.230
They can't be negative.

00:08:35.230 --> 00:08:38.380
And what we'll have is
we have a bunch of foods.

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:41.299
So food j, when you
get 1 unit of it--

00:08:41.299 --> 00:08:44.490
doesn't matter what a
unit is, but 1 unit of it,

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:48.120
is going to have an
amount aij of nutrient i.

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:50.970
So we care about a
bunch of nutrients,

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:54.990
like your maybe 10 or 20 or
5 or something like that.

00:08:54.990 --> 00:08:57.030
Various nutrients.

00:08:57.030 --> 00:09:02.420
And a healthy diet is
going to require nutrient i

00:09:02.420 --> 00:09:04.730
in at least a quantity bi.

00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:06.680
So that's your--
let's say that's

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:10.340
your minimum daily advised blah,
blah, blah, whatever it is.

00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:10.880
OK.

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:14.210
So then immediately-- well,
not quite immediately,

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:16.520
but I'll explain something here.

00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:18.620
So to find the
cheapest healthy diet,

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:22.760
you minimize-- that's the
cost of the foodstuffs.

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:24.570
And then you could
even annotate--

00:09:24.570 --> 00:09:26.750
you can imagine annotating
the constraints, which

00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:27.350
would be really good.

00:09:27.350 --> 00:09:29.267
So if I annotated that,
that's not a concern--

00:09:29.267 --> 00:09:32.060
I'd annotate that that's
the cost, total cost.

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:35.210
This says the amounts
have to be non--

00:09:35.210 --> 00:09:38.360
they have to be-- they
can't be negative.

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:41.690
And each line of this you
could annotate and say,

00:09:41.690 --> 00:09:43.610
this line says that's
the minimum amount

00:09:43.610 --> 00:09:45.740
of protein in a diet.

00:09:45.740 --> 00:09:48.380
That's the--
something like that.

00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:51.020
Now technically speaking,
if you look at this

00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:54.680
and then compare it to what
we defined in LP to be,

00:09:54.680 --> 00:09:59.090
like this, it's not the same.

00:09:59.090 --> 00:10:03.920
But here, we're going to start
doing things where once--

00:10:03.920 --> 00:10:06.740
I think at this point in the
class and moving forward,

00:10:06.740 --> 00:10:11.000
you can do a couple of quick
transformations of the problem

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:12.410
without even mentioning it.

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:15.200
So anyone would look at
that and say, that's an LP.

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:16.940
It doesn't have
exactly the right form.

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:18.170
There's a few silly things.

00:10:18.170 --> 00:10:22.280
Like, for example, here,
the inequalities are--

00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:23.280
they go the wrong way.

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:24.905
In fact, both of them
go the wrong way.

00:10:24.905 --> 00:10:27.260
So I'll just do this once
and then never again.

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:32.720
So what's going to happen is
you have Ax bigger than b.

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:36.080
And also, x is bigger than 0.

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:38.780
And then we're going to write--
we're going to end up writing

00:10:38.780 --> 00:10:39.590
that--

00:10:39.590 --> 00:10:41.640
let me get my
notation down here.

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:44.330
So the notation
is Gx less than h.

00:10:44.330 --> 00:10:51.680
So we're going to somehow
write this as Gx less than h.

00:10:51.680 --> 00:10:55.230
Anyway, anybody got
some suggestions?

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:55.980
Minus sign.

00:10:55.980 --> 00:10:56.940
Yeah, minus sign.

00:10:56.940 --> 00:10:58.170
I like it.

00:10:58.170 --> 00:10:59.850
So we'll start by doing this.

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:03.270
We'll have minus
A. So this thing

00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:09.090
is equivalent to minus Ax is
less than or equal to minus b.

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:12.840
And this one is
equivalent to this.

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:14.312
0 is the same as minus 0.

00:11:14.312 --> 00:11:15.270
Everybody cool on that?

00:11:15.270 --> 00:11:16.590
That was step 1.

00:11:16.590 --> 00:11:19.590
Then step 2 is you just stack.

00:11:19.590 --> 00:11:22.740
So you just do this.

00:11:22.740 --> 00:11:27.330
And that's less than b and 0.

00:11:27.330 --> 00:11:30.270
So here's G and here's h.

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:32.490
So everybody got that?

00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:34.020
So yeah, we'll
never do that again.

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:36.340
I'll never do that again,
let's put it that way.

00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:38.940
And the truth is, you
don't have to either

00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:43.680
because modern languages,
which you'll be using shortly

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:46.800
to do this, will do
all this stuff for you,

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:48.030
so you don't have to know--

00:11:48.030 --> 00:11:49.830
But in the old
days-- or actually,

00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:51.180
every now and then you
might meet a colleague--

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:52.860
you might be at an internship
or something like that,

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:55.402
find someone who's using linear
programming or something like

00:11:55.402 --> 00:11:56.980
that, and they would--

00:11:56.980 --> 00:11:59.360
they'd say, how long
did it take you to do?

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:01.300
And they would say,
oh, it's a huge pain.

00:12:01.300 --> 00:12:03.760
It took me like weeks
and weeks and weeks

00:12:03.760 --> 00:12:07.720
because they had to
convert their problem to--

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:09.250
they had to do
this kind of stuff

00:12:09.250 --> 00:12:11.530
and keep track of
everything, and then

00:12:11.530 --> 00:12:13.840
to debug that is
not fun and so on.

00:12:13.840 --> 00:12:18.790
We refer to that, I guess,
derisively as matrix stuffing.

00:12:18.790 --> 00:12:21.090
So a question?

00:12:21.090 --> 00:12:23.465
Would this be considered
an identical or equivalent

00:12:23.465 --> 00:12:23.965
problem?

00:12:23.965 --> 00:12:25.270
Ah, OK.

00:12:25.270 --> 00:12:25.990
Good question.

00:12:25.990 --> 00:12:27.880
These are actually
equivalent, yeah.

00:12:27.880 --> 00:12:29.590
Because-- yeah.

00:12:29.590 --> 00:12:32.410
Actually-- I think, actually,
that doesn't even fit

00:12:32.410 --> 00:12:33.220
our general--

00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:35.080
our general form
was very restricted.

00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:39.280
It was like fi is less than
or equal to 0 and stuff.

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:42.340
But for sure, even
if it's-- yeah,

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:44.800
when you do these small
transformations which keep

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:47.917
everything equivalent, the two--

00:12:47.917 --> 00:12:50.125
the thing you transform
something or reduce something

00:12:50.125 --> 00:12:53.890
to, to use the computer science
term, is not the same problem,

00:12:53.890 --> 00:12:56.130
it is equivalent.

00:12:56.130 --> 00:12:59.040
Yeah, so OK.

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:00.900
All right, so back to--

00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:02.790
oh, so that's our--

00:13:02.790 --> 00:13:05.430
here's our healthiest
diet thing.

00:13:05.430 --> 00:13:07.365
What if I had an upper
limit on a nutrient?

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:15.720
I mean, could we handle it?

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:16.220
Yeah.

00:13:16.220 --> 00:13:17.512
It's kind of something similar.

00:13:17.512 --> 00:13:19.580
Like, the answer is,
sure, that would be-- we

00:13:19.580 --> 00:13:21.020
could certainly do that.

00:13:21.020 --> 00:13:25.640
What if there were like supply
availability issues and some

00:13:25.640 --> 00:13:26.870
of these goods--

00:13:26.870 --> 00:13:28.490
some of these foods
are only available

00:13:28.490 --> 00:13:31.587
up to some current
limit of supply?

00:13:31.587 --> 00:13:32.920
Of course you could handle that.

00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:34.870
So once you start figuring
out how to do this,

00:13:34.870 --> 00:13:37.230
you can see that
it's very expressive.

00:13:37.230 --> 00:13:39.270
There's a lot of stuff
you can do with it.

00:13:39.270 --> 00:13:42.518
So this is just a very
famous historical example.

00:13:42.518 --> 00:13:44.310
I think it's probably
one of the first ones

00:13:44.310 --> 00:13:47.730
that people ever talked about.

00:13:47.730 --> 00:13:49.400
OK.

00:13:49.400 --> 00:13:52.280
Next up for fun is piecewise
linear minimization.

00:13:52.280 --> 00:13:54.500
So this is a piecewise
linear function

00:13:54.500 --> 00:13:57.380
expressed as the maximum of
a bunch of affine functions.

00:13:57.380 --> 00:13:59.870
So each one of those has a
graph that's like a plane,

00:13:59.870 --> 00:14:01.640
and then you just
intersect them,

00:14:01.640 --> 00:14:04.100
and that's your piecewise
linear function.

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:06.890
And then you want
to minimize it.

00:14:06.890 --> 00:14:10.310
And so the way this would
work is if I walk up to you

00:14:10.310 --> 00:14:12.140
and I said, is that an LP?

00:14:12.140 --> 00:14:14.930
You would have to
say no because--

00:14:14.930 --> 00:14:17.847
I mean, to check if something
is an LP, or a linear program,

00:14:17.847 --> 00:14:19.430
the first thing you
do is you check is

00:14:19.430 --> 00:14:21.710
the objective linear or affine.

00:14:21.710 --> 00:14:23.120
It's not.

00:14:23.120 --> 00:14:28.520
I mean, if m is more
than 1, it's not affine.

00:14:28.520 --> 00:14:32.480
But in fact, this easily
converts to an LP.

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:34.340
And you do that by
the epigraph form.

00:14:34.340 --> 00:14:36.980
So you just introduce
a new variable t here.

00:14:36.980 --> 00:14:41.330
And then these constraints
here basically say

00:14:41.330 --> 00:14:44.238
that xt is in the
epigraph of that function.

00:14:44.238 --> 00:14:45.780
Or you can just
think of it this way.

00:14:45.780 --> 00:14:47.420
It just says that
t is an upper bound

00:14:47.420 --> 00:14:49.450
on the max of these things.

00:14:49.450 --> 00:14:51.690
So this says-- so
this would typically--

00:14:51.690 --> 00:14:54.390
this would actually-- this
problem here says minimize

00:14:54.390 --> 00:14:59.190
the upper bound on the max,
but also mess with the x's.

00:14:59.190 --> 00:15:01.350
And so you can quickly argue--

00:15:01.350 --> 00:15:02.748
you could easily-- it's not--

00:15:02.748 --> 00:15:04.290
I mean, someone has
to do it, but you

00:15:04.290 --> 00:15:06.360
could show that these
are actually equivalent.

00:15:06.360 --> 00:15:08.460
Actually, quite specifically,
if you solve that,

00:15:08.460 --> 00:15:10.890
that's an LP, that's an
honest LP right there.

00:15:10.890 --> 00:15:15.500
That is a linear objective, and
these are linear constraints.

00:15:15.500 --> 00:15:16.550
OK, fine.

00:15:16.550 --> 00:15:19.550
If you wanted to, put
the t on the other side.

00:15:19.550 --> 00:15:21.770
But that's in that
thing and I'm not

00:15:21.770 --> 00:15:23.240
going to show that
type of thing.

00:15:23.240 --> 00:15:24.290
Everybody got this?

00:15:24.290 --> 00:15:26.850
So it's kind of cool.

00:15:26.850 --> 00:15:29.990
And this already is
actually kind of useful,

00:15:29.990 --> 00:15:33.260
just the ability-- and
by the way, most people--

00:15:33.260 --> 00:15:35.750
let's see-- that are actually
doing this kind of stuff,

00:15:35.750 --> 00:15:39.860
if you came up with this and
someone said, well, cool,

00:15:39.860 --> 00:15:41.030
but how do I solve that?

00:15:41.030 --> 00:15:43.820
People just look at you
and go like, it's an LP.

00:15:43.820 --> 00:15:46.460
So people would expect
you to know that.

00:15:46.460 --> 00:15:48.530
Or cool people would anyway.

00:15:48.530 --> 00:15:49.940
Other people would--

00:15:49.940 --> 00:15:52.790
I don't-- God only knows
what they would do.

00:15:52.790 --> 00:15:55.250
I do know some of the
things they would do.

00:15:55.250 --> 00:15:56.640
OK.

00:15:56.640 --> 00:15:57.140
OK.

00:15:57.140 --> 00:15:59.270
So we'll look at
another example, again,

00:15:59.270 --> 00:16:00.530
of linear programming.

00:16:00.530 --> 00:16:07.010
Actually, I think it's actually
our first glimpse at this issue

00:16:07.010 --> 00:16:09.260
of finding a point that's--
actually, it's our second.

00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:12.560
Find a point that's central
that's deep inside a set,

00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:13.340
like a center.

00:16:13.340 --> 00:16:14.840
I think we talked
about-- we already

00:16:14.840 --> 00:16:16.215
found-- we already
encountered it

00:16:16.215 --> 00:16:17.780
once it was the yield center.

00:16:17.780 --> 00:16:22.030
It says, here's your here's
your region of stuff that works,

00:16:22.030 --> 00:16:24.850
dial up the machines
to get a target so

00:16:24.850 --> 00:16:26.260
that your yield is maximized.

00:16:26.260 --> 00:16:28.480
So that was one
notion of the center.

00:16:28.480 --> 00:16:30.580
This is another one,
is a Chebyshev center.

00:16:30.580 --> 00:16:34.570
So here's my
polyhedron described

00:16:34.570 --> 00:16:36.730
by linear inequalities.

00:16:36.730 --> 00:16:42.210
And that your job is this.

00:16:42.210 --> 00:16:44.780
You want to find the
largest inscribed ball.

00:16:44.780 --> 00:16:47.210
Inscribed means it's
inside the polyhedron.

00:16:47.210 --> 00:16:49.400
And you want--
basically, you want

00:16:49.400 --> 00:16:52.520
to find the largest ball that
fits inside the polyhedron.

00:16:52.520 --> 00:16:53.660
Everybody got it?

00:16:53.660 --> 00:16:55.490
So that's the question.

00:16:55.490 --> 00:16:59.060
Let me just mention one
thing right here because it's

00:16:59.060 --> 00:17:02.020
probably worth mentioning.

00:17:02.020 --> 00:17:04.270
If someone comes up
to you and says--

00:17:04.270 --> 00:17:06.970
I mean, if we're talking
about linear programming,

00:17:06.970 --> 00:17:08.990
then here are the things--

00:17:08.990 --> 00:17:11.740
first of all, a part of
your brain should light up.

00:17:11.740 --> 00:17:13.300
On the geometric
side, you should

00:17:13.300 --> 00:17:17.109
see pictures of flat
functions-- affine functions.

00:17:17.109 --> 00:17:19.720
Maybe you should also see
pictures of piecewise affine

00:17:19.720 --> 00:17:20.560
functions.

00:17:20.560 --> 00:17:22.869
Those are the things
that should light up

00:17:22.869 --> 00:17:25.869
when I say linear programming
to you in your brain.

00:17:25.869 --> 00:17:26.770
We know this.

00:17:26.770 --> 00:17:29.560
We actually do take students,
put them in MRI machines,

00:17:29.560 --> 00:17:31.810
and check these things.

00:17:31.810 --> 00:17:34.300
Actually, we don't,
but we could.

00:17:34.300 --> 00:17:38.020
If we did, then every time after
that, I could actually honestly

00:17:38.020 --> 00:17:39.130
say we did this.

00:17:39.130 --> 00:17:40.600
So I may even think--

00:17:40.600 --> 00:17:42.190
I'll think about it.

00:17:42.190 --> 00:17:43.690
OK.

00:17:43.690 --> 00:17:44.260
All right.

00:17:44.260 --> 00:17:50.760
So what I was saying is that
the minute you get to 2-norm,

00:17:50.760 --> 00:17:54.400
a rather different part of
your brain should light up.

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:56.980
Because when I
say 2-norm to you,

00:17:56.980 --> 00:17:59.110
visually, here's what
should be showing up.

00:17:59.110 --> 00:18:00.430
Things that are round.

00:18:00.430 --> 00:18:04.610
Balls, Euclidean
distances, which should not

00:18:04.610 --> 00:18:07.035
be stored anywhere near
the part of your brain

00:18:07.035 --> 00:18:09.410
that lights up when I say
linear program, which should be

00:18:09.410 --> 00:18:12.350
piecewise linear things, flat
things, affine things, that

00:18:12.350 --> 00:18:13.850
kind of-- everybody
following this?

00:18:13.850 --> 00:18:17.060
So honestly, when you're
listening and you see that

00:18:17.060 --> 00:18:20.860
and you just see this
ball here, you should say,

00:18:20.860 --> 00:18:25.360
there's no way that something
involving a quadratic

00:18:25.360 --> 00:18:27.340
is going to end up
as a linear program.

00:18:27.340 --> 00:18:30.220
I'm just-- I mean,
obviously it does

00:18:30.220 --> 00:18:31.820
because it's right
here on the slide,

00:18:31.820 --> 00:18:34.000
but the point is,
I'm just saying

00:18:34.000 --> 00:18:35.560
it's a little bit surprising.

00:18:35.560 --> 00:18:37.430
So, OK.

00:18:37.430 --> 00:18:37.930
All right.

00:18:37.930 --> 00:18:39.050
So how do you solve that?

00:18:39.050 --> 00:18:40.660
How do you find the--

00:18:40.660 --> 00:18:43.990
how do you find the
ball, the largest ball

00:18:43.990 --> 00:18:45.910
that fits inside a polyhedron?

00:18:45.910 --> 00:18:47.620
Well, here's what you do.

00:18:47.620 --> 00:18:49.360
What you do-- the
first thing you ask is,

00:18:49.360 --> 00:18:53.350
when is it true that a
ball is in a half-space?

00:18:53.350 --> 00:18:55.900
Because then to say a
ball is in a polyhedron

00:18:55.900 --> 00:18:57.840
is to say it's in all
of these half-spaces

00:18:57.840 --> 00:18:59.590
because the polyhedron
is the intersection

00:18:59.590 --> 00:19:00.370
of the half-spaces.

00:19:00.370 --> 00:19:00.870
OK.

00:19:00.870 --> 00:19:02.930
So when is a ball
in a half-space?

00:19:02.930 --> 00:19:05.180
And the answer is interesting.

00:19:05.180 --> 00:19:08.680
We parametrize the ball by
its center and its radius.

00:19:08.680 --> 00:19:11.470
That's a vector and
not a positive scalar.

00:19:11.470 --> 00:19:12.350
OK?

00:19:12.350 --> 00:19:12.850
OK.

00:19:12.850 --> 00:19:15.280
So here it is.

00:19:15.280 --> 00:19:16.870
When is it true--
what you want to do

00:19:16.870 --> 00:19:20.030
is you want to maximize
this over the ball,

00:19:20.030 --> 00:19:22.910
and you want that maximum
to be less than bi.

00:19:22.910 --> 00:19:24.200
But that we can solve.

00:19:24.200 --> 00:19:27.530
If I ask you to maximize an
inner product over a ball,

00:19:27.530 --> 00:19:28.400
it's easy.

00:19:28.400 --> 00:19:30.855
We can do that exactly
because it's-- first of all,

00:19:30.855 --> 00:19:32.480
there's the
Cauchy-Schwarz-- basically,

00:19:32.480 --> 00:19:34.105
the Cauchy-Schwarz
inequality is tight.

00:19:34.105 --> 00:19:35.870
That's what happens.

00:19:35.870 --> 00:19:39.350
And so it turns out,
this thing, that's

00:19:39.350 --> 00:19:44.360
the maximum value of this
thing over a ball of this-- oh,

00:19:44.360 --> 00:19:45.582
sorry, yeah, that's right.

00:19:45.582 --> 00:19:47.540
That's the maximum value
of this over the ball,

00:19:47.540 --> 00:19:49.940
and it's equal to
ai transpose xc.

00:19:49.940 --> 00:19:50.990
That's this term.

00:19:50.990 --> 00:19:53.750
Plus-- and then over here,
we have ai transpose u,

00:19:53.750 --> 00:19:55.190
but u is less than r.

00:19:55.190 --> 00:19:57.440
And so by Cauchy-Schwarz,
that's-- definitely

00:19:57.440 --> 00:19:59.100
that's an upper
bound, but in fact,

00:19:59.100 --> 00:20:02.300
it's equal if I take
u the right way.

00:20:02.300 --> 00:20:07.310
So in fact, u would be
ai appropriately scaled.

00:20:07.310 --> 00:20:10.550
So it says that you get--

00:20:10.550 --> 00:20:17.930
that b is a subset of p if and
only if this holds for each i.

00:20:17.930 --> 00:20:19.910
So that's this constraint here.

00:20:19.910 --> 00:20:24.480
Now when you look at this,
again, you see a 2-norm here.

00:20:24.480 --> 00:20:27.530
And so there should be like at
least a yellow light flashing

00:20:27.530 --> 00:20:28.190
somewhere.

00:20:28.190 --> 00:20:31.340
Like this is not going to be an
LP because it's got a 2-norm.

00:20:31.340 --> 00:20:34.850
It'd be the same if there was
an x or a sine or a cosine.

00:20:34.850 --> 00:20:37.490
You would say, doesn't
sound like LP to me.

00:20:37.490 --> 00:20:39.920
But you just gotta
be very careful here

00:20:39.920 --> 00:20:41.630
and take a look at this.

00:20:41.630 --> 00:20:44.960
The variables are r and xc.

00:20:44.960 --> 00:20:47.360
And the objective is what?

00:20:47.360 --> 00:20:47.990
Is linear.

00:20:47.990 --> 00:20:48.747
That's cool.

00:20:48.747 --> 00:20:50.330
And then we look at
these constraints.

00:20:50.330 --> 00:20:52.760
Again, the 2-norm
is causing this

00:20:52.760 --> 00:20:56.390
either discomfort or yellow
flashing light, which

00:20:56.390 --> 00:20:59.660
is warning, warning, may not
be a-- need not be LP ahead.

00:20:59.660 --> 00:21:03.570
But that's a-- ai is given data.

00:21:03.570 --> 00:21:05.240
It describes a face
of the polyhedron,

00:21:05.240 --> 00:21:07.940
so that's just a
positive number.

00:21:07.940 --> 00:21:10.790
What kind of constraint
is that in the variables,

00:21:10.790 --> 00:21:12.924
which are xc and r?

00:21:12.924 --> 00:21:13.830
Linear.

00:21:13.830 --> 00:21:15.480
It's just linear.

00:21:15.480 --> 00:21:18.240
So-- I mean, maybe I made
too big a deal out of them.

00:21:18.240 --> 00:21:21.150
Anyway, so by the way,
practical conclusion

00:21:21.150 --> 00:21:25.620
is you can solve this problem
even at enormous scales.

00:21:25.620 --> 00:21:26.880
So there you go.

00:21:26.880 --> 00:21:30.150
By the way, is the Chebyshev
center of a polyhedron,

00:21:30.150 --> 00:21:32.770
is it unique?

00:21:32.770 --> 00:21:35.000
Is it?

00:21:35.000 --> 00:21:36.920
I see some people saying no.

00:21:36.920 --> 00:21:37.880
How come?

00:21:37.880 --> 00:21:38.943
What's an example?

00:21:38.943 --> 00:21:40.610
The rectangle would
have a [INAUDIBLE]..

00:21:40.610 --> 00:21:41.420
I like it.

00:21:41.420 --> 00:21:44.818
OK, so here's a rectangle.

00:21:44.818 --> 00:21:46.610
This is going to come
out terribly, but OK.

00:21:46.610 --> 00:21:49.670
That's a-- if that were
inside, that would have been

00:21:49.670 --> 00:21:52.560
a Chebyshev-- that would
have been a Chebyshev--

00:21:52.560 --> 00:21:54.560
solution to a Chebyshev
problem, but so is this.

00:21:57.320 --> 00:22:02.470
So anyway, so it's not.

00:22:02.470 --> 00:22:05.980
By the way, this is
already quite useful

00:22:05.980 --> 00:22:08.140
here because it's already--

00:22:08.140 --> 00:22:11.230
I mean, this already has
just immediate applications.

00:22:11.230 --> 00:22:12.520
So, OK.

00:22:15.110 --> 00:22:20.550
Next up, I think we're in around
1950 or something like that.

00:22:20.550 --> 00:22:23.120
So here we are.

00:22:23.120 --> 00:22:25.490
This is-- yeah, so part of
this is also historical,

00:22:25.490 --> 00:22:27.800
but it's not bad to
know these names.

00:22:27.800 --> 00:22:29.360
So here's one.

00:22:29.360 --> 00:22:33.110
So linear fractional program,
that looks like this.

00:22:33.110 --> 00:22:35.360
You minimize a linear
fractional function.

00:22:35.360 --> 00:22:40.070
That's a ratio of two
affine functions where--

00:22:40.070 --> 00:22:41.780
actually, by
convention, we assume

00:22:41.780 --> 00:22:45.350
the domain is where the
denominator is positive.

00:22:45.350 --> 00:22:47.000
So that's what that is.

00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:49.100
And then just over a polyhedron.

00:22:49.100 --> 00:22:50.250
That's all.

00:22:50.250 --> 00:22:50.750
OK.

00:22:50.750 --> 00:22:53.120
Now it's a quasiconvex
problem, and we just

00:22:53.120 --> 00:22:54.780
talked about how to solve them.

00:22:54.780 --> 00:22:58.640
So you can always solve a
linear fractional problem

00:22:58.640 --> 00:23:00.650
by doing bisection
where you're calling

00:23:00.650 --> 00:23:03.290
an LP solver in each step.

00:23:03.290 --> 00:23:05.960
So we just-- that's part
of the generic thing.

00:23:05.960 --> 00:23:09.050
But what's cool here-- and
this was figured out maybe also

00:23:09.050 --> 00:23:10.940
in maybe the '50s
or early '60s--

00:23:10.940 --> 00:23:12.860
I don't remember exactly.

00:23:12.860 --> 00:23:15.943
It turns out, you can solve
this problem as a single LP.

00:23:15.943 --> 00:23:16.860
And I'm not going to--

00:23:16.860 --> 00:23:19.500
I won't get into the
details, but it's

00:23:19.500 --> 00:23:21.940
something where you can
look in the book, look here,

00:23:21.940 --> 00:23:25.740
it's not totally
obvious how to do this.

00:23:25.740 --> 00:23:27.480
Yeah, you introduce
some new variables.

00:23:27.480 --> 00:23:30.660
I think I won't go into it
and you reparameterize--

00:23:30.660 --> 00:23:32.640
and in fact, it has to
do with this perspective

00:23:32.640 --> 00:23:33.500
transformation.

00:23:33.500 --> 00:23:34.750
I'm not going to say anything.

00:23:34.750 --> 00:23:36.375
So by the way, you
should not-- there's

00:23:36.375 --> 00:23:39.510
no way a person can
look at that with this

00:23:39.510 --> 00:23:42.330
and then say, look at this and
go, oh, yeah, no, I totally

00:23:42.330 --> 00:23:42.880
see it.

00:23:42.880 --> 00:23:45.600
So this is an example of
a problem transformation

00:23:45.600 --> 00:23:48.320
that's not obvious.

00:23:48.320 --> 00:23:51.430
And I think I'm not going to
take the time to go into it.

00:23:51.430 --> 00:23:53.890
So the others have
all been obvious.

00:23:53.890 --> 00:23:56.770
Like you can take an
inequality that's bigger than

00:23:56.770 --> 00:23:58.660
or equal to, multiply
everything by minus 1,

00:23:58.660 --> 00:24:01.040
and now it's a less
than or equal to.

00:24:01.040 --> 00:24:02.785
This one is not.

00:24:02.785 --> 00:24:04.660
Actually, there's a
whole bunch that are not,

00:24:04.660 --> 00:24:05.470
which we'll get to.

00:24:05.470 --> 00:24:06.670
OK.

00:24:06.670 --> 00:24:10.192
Now a generalization of
that is the so-called--

00:24:10.192 --> 00:24:12.400
people call it generalized
linear fractional problem.

00:24:12.400 --> 00:24:14.320
That's a maximum of
a bunch of these.

00:24:14.320 --> 00:24:17.230
Are actually quasiconvex,
and the maximum

00:24:17.230 --> 00:24:19.510
of a bunch of quasi convex
functions is quasiconvex.

00:24:19.510 --> 00:24:20.800
So that's quasiconvex.

00:24:20.800 --> 00:24:23.740
And in fact, for this problem,
the only way to solve it

00:24:23.740 --> 00:24:26.750
is, in fact, by bisection,
as far as I know.

00:24:26.750 --> 00:24:27.990
I mean, not that it matters.

00:24:27.990 --> 00:24:29.070
But, OK.

00:24:29.070 --> 00:24:32.190
And here's a very famous example
of-- it's from Von Neumann.

00:24:32.190 --> 00:24:36.960
And it's the Von Neumann
model of a growing economy.

00:24:36.960 --> 00:24:38.610
So let me explain what it is.

00:24:38.610 --> 00:24:42.000
We have two sets of
variables, x and x plus.

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:44.040
And each of those,
they're non-negative,

00:24:44.040 --> 00:24:47.638
and they represent--

00:24:47.638 --> 00:24:48.430
hey, wait a minute.

00:24:48.430 --> 00:24:50.170
Do we have x--

00:24:50.170 --> 00:24:53.530
hey, shouldn't I have x bigger
than or equal to 0 here?

00:24:56.210 --> 00:24:57.410
Yeah, we should.

00:24:57.410 --> 00:25:00.350
Or maybe-- oh,
maybe it's absorbed

00:25:00.350 --> 00:25:01.920
in the denominator of this.

00:25:01.920 --> 00:25:02.420
Sorry.

00:25:02.420 --> 00:25:04.130
OK, I confused
myself momentarily.

00:25:04.130 --> 00:25:07.160
My confusion is over now,
though, so everything's cool.

00:25:07.160 --> 00:25:09.230
OK.

00:25:09.230 --> 00:25:10.400
Here it is.

00:25:10.400 --> 00:25:10.970
OK.

00:25:10.970 --> 00:25:14.570
So x plus is the
economic activity

00:25:14.570 --> 00:25:17.670
across n sectors of an economy.

00:25:17.670 --> 00:25:20.190
x is the current
economic activity

00:25:20.190 --> 00:25:24.540
across n sectors in an economy.

00:25:24.540 --> 00:25:28.890
xi plus over xi is
literally the growth

00:25:28.890 --> 00:25:30.640
in that sector of the economy.

00:25:30.640 --> 00:25:36.090
So you would say, I don't know,
steel manufacturing went up 6%,

00:25:36.090 --> 00:25:38.760
it means that this
number is 1.06.

00:25:38.760 --> 00:25:44.580
If it's 0.93, it means that
it actually contracted 7%.

00:25:44.580 --> 00:25:45.820
Everybody got it?

00:25:45.820 --> 00:25:46.320
OK.

00:25:46.320 --> 00:25:47.560
So here's what we want.

00:25:47.560 --> 00:25:50.220
We want to figure
out, what would

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:52.770
be the right economic
activity levels

00:25:52.770 --> 00:25:58.800
to maximize the minimum growth
in sectors across the economy?

00:25:58.800 --> 00:25:59.775
That's this.

00:26:03.240 --> 00:26:03.810
OK.

00:26:03.810 --> 00:26:04.860
Here are the constraints.

00:26:04.860 --> 00:26:07.750
Here's how x and x
plus are constrained.

00:26:07.750 --> 00:26:09.390
They're constrained this way.

00:26:09.390 --> 00:26:10.710
We have two matrices.

00:26:10.710 --> 00:26:20.850
We have a number of goods that
are consumed and also produced

00:26:20.850 --> 00:26:22.590
by economic activity.

00:26:22.590 --> 00:26:25.710
So one unit of economic
activity in sector i

00:26:25.710 --> 00:26:27.953
might consume a bunch of things.

00:26:27.953 --> 00:26:30.120
Actually, it's going to
have positive and negative--

00:26:30.120 --> 00:26:33.300
because if it's consuming,
it'll have all sorts of stuff

00:26:33.300 --> 00:26:36.630
that it's maybe consuming,
but it's also producing stuff.

00:26:36.630 --> 00:26:38.760
Like manufacturing
automobiles, you

00:26:38.760 --> 00:26:42.960
have-- it consumes steel
and various other things,

00:26:42.960 --> 00:26:46.920
and then it produces cars,
like just as an example.

00:26:46.920 --> 00:26:48.180
OK.

00:26:48.180 --> 00:26:50.940
So what this says is--

00:26:50.940 --> 00:26:57.060
so Ax, A-- when you multiply
the matrix A by the vector x,

00:26:57.060 --> 00:26:59.940
what you get is the vector of--

00:26:59.940 --> 00:27:04.120
you get what is
produced this year.

00:27:04.120 --> 00:27:06.690
That's the set of goods that
were produced this year.

00:27:06.690 --> 00:27:08.933
It would be-- so-- and the
model here is very simple,

00:27:08.933 --> 00:27:09.600
that-- whatever.

00:27:09.600 --> 00:27:12.240
If this is year, quarters,
or months, it doesn't matter.

00:27:12.240 --> 00:27:14.310
The idea is that what's
produced this year

00:27:14.310 --> 00:27:18.040
we imagine is not immediately
fed into various--

00:27:18.040 --> 00:27:21.410
but it's almost as if-- well,
we do this in discrete time.

00:27:21.410 --> 00:27:24.280
So what's produced now is
held in escrow for a year,

00:27:24.280 --> 00:27:27.670
and then on January 1,
everything starts up again

00:27:27.670 --> 00:27:28.640
and you use those.

00:27:28.640 --> 00:27:32.020
And so what this says is
that's the vector of resources

00:27:32.020 --> 00:27:35.210
consumed by economic
activity next year,

00:27:35.210 --> 00:27:38.320
and it says that is less
than or equal to what

00:27:38.320 --> 00:27:39.820
you produced this year.

00:27:39.820 --> 00:27:42.490
And there's tons of
variations on this.

00:27:42.490 --> 00:27:45.610
We could do all sorts
of variations on this.

00:27:45.610 --> 00:27:48.530
We could have, for example, we
could do this for 10 periods

00:27:48.530 --> 00:27:50.200
and have a running--

00:27:50.200 --> 00:27:53.290
a stockpile of stuff.

00:27:53.290 --> 00:27:54.460
So, OK.

00:27:54.460 --> 00:27:55.600
But this is the basic one.

00:27:55.600 --> 00:27:56.750
Makes sense?

00:27:56.750 --> 00:27:57.250
OK.

00:27:57.250 --> 00:28:01.570
And this is, just immediately,
a generalized linear fractional

00:28:01.570 --> 00:28:02.260
problem.

00:28:02.260 --> 00:28:03.040
A few things here.

00:28:03.040 --> 00:28:05.740
I'm maximizing a min,
whereas this thing

00:28:05.740 --> 00:28:10.360
should say minimize a maximum.

00:28:10.360 --> 00:28:13.630
But you just flip the sign
and everything's cool.

00:28:13.630 --> 00:28:14.590
Everybody got it?

00:28:14.590 --> 00:28:16.300
And by the way,
things like this--

00:28:16.300 --> 00:28:17.770
these are not obvious things.

00:28:17.770 --> 00:28:20.207
I guarantee you,
with 10 sectors,

00:28:20.207 --> 00:28:21.790
you could not figure
this out by hand.

00:28:21.790 --> 00:28:24.910
I mean, they're just
absolutely out of the question.

00:28:24.910 --> 00:28:28.330
So-- I mean, but of course,
we can solve a problem

00:28:28.330 --> 00:28:33.130
like that with 10,000 sectors
with no problem at all.

00:28:33.130 --> 00:28:35.890
Like, you could, let
me put it that way.

00:28:35.890 --> 00:28:37.720
So everybody got this?

00:28:37.720 --> 00:28:40.600
So it's kind of cool.

00:28:40.600 --> 00:28:42.430
OK.

00:28:42.430 --> 00:28:46.240
Next up, we're
probably early 1950s,

00:28:46.240 --> 00:28:48.970
is the so-called
quadratic program.

00:28:48.970 --> 00:28:50.680
This is like linear programming.

00:28:50.680 --> 00:28:52.990
This is actually a
problem class that you

00:28:52.990 --> 00:28:57.100
need to know about just
because it's a thing.

00:28:57.100 --> 00:28:58.150
People talk about it.

00:28:58.150 --> 00:29:01.708
I guarantee, when you go various
places, I don't care the field,

00:29:01.708 --> 00:29:03.250
someone will say,
how do you do that?

00:29:03.250 --> 00:29:05.890
And someone will say, it's
a QP or something like that.

00:29:05.890 --> 00:29:09.370
So quadratic program is this.

00:29:09.370 --> 00:29:12.670
The constraints are still
linear just like an LP.

00:29:12.670 --> 00:29:13.693
So we have a--

00:29:13.693 --> 00:29:15.490
a polyhedron is
your feasible set.

00:29:15.490 --> 00:29:18.940
But the objective is
a convex quadratic.

00:29:18.940 --> 00:29:20.230
So that that's the idea.

00:29:20.230 --> 00:29:22.300
And here, the only thing
that changes is here's

00:29:22.300 --> 00:29:24.100
your polyhedron, which is your--

00:29:24.100 --> 00:29:24.640
this thing.

00:29:24.640 --> 00:29:28.420
And the sublevel sets, remember,
in an LP, they're just--

00:29:28.420 --> 00:29:32.140
not sublevel sets-- well,
sublevel sets are half-spaces.

00:29:32.140 --> 00:29:33.970
The level sets were hyperplanes.

00:29:33.970 --> 00:29:39.010
Now the level sets are actually
the surfaces of ellipsoids--

00:29:39.010 --> 00:29:40.660
I mean, if p is
positive definite.

00:29:40.660 --> 00:29:42.770
And if it's not, then
it's the surface of a--

00:29:42.770 --> 00:29:43.260
I don't know, whatever.

00:29:43.260 --> 00:29:44.677
I don't know what
people call it--

00:29:44.677 --> 00:29:47.360
degenerate ellipsoid
or something like that.

00:29:47.360 --> 00:29:49.460
And this is the picture.

00:29:49.460 --> 00:29:55.565
You want to go downhill here
to the minimum while staying

00:29:55.565 --> 00:29:57.690
in here, and here, you'll
find out it's right here.

00:29:57.690 --> 00:30:00.023
And actually, you can see our
optimality condition here.

00:30:00.023 --> 00:30:03.600
That when you're optimal,
the negative gradient,

00:30:03.600 --> 00:30:07.940
which is pointing you towards
the unconstrained minimum

00:30:07.940 --> 00:30:11.360
roughly here, is actually
an outward normal

00:30:11.360 --> 00:30:13.340
of the feasible set.

00:30:13.340 --> 00:30:15.290
And by the way, if
that were not the case,

00:30:15.290 --> 00:30:19.160
I could wiggle x and
do a little bit better.

00:30:19.160 --> 00:30:20.190
Everybody got that?

00:30:20.190 --> 00:30:21.680
So this is a good picture.

00:30:21.680 --> 00:30:22.970
I mean, what it doesn't--

00:30:22.970 --> 00:30:25.130
what this doesn't
tell you is that--

00:30:25.130 --> 00:30:26.630
I mean, and the
point here, it's a--

00:30:26.630 --> 00:30:29.360
you should have this picture
in your mind for sure.

00:30:29.360 --> 00:30:33.410
But I guess the main point here
is that we can solve QPs with--

00:30:33.410 --> 00:30:36.680
you can solve QPs with
50,000 variables and it

00:30:36.680 --> 00:30:37.640
just-- it's nothing.

00:30:37.640 --> 00:30:38.750
It's just nothing.

00:30:38.750 --> 00:30:41.600
It can be done so reliably
that you can actually

00:30:41.600 --> 00:30:43.550
have this embedded
in control systems

00:30:43.550 --> 00:30:46.340
and have this done 100 times
a second with a failure

00:30:46.340 --> 00:30:49.250
rate of 0.

00:30:49.250 --> 00:30:50.840
Just-- OK.

00:30:50.840 --> 00:30:52.400
So that's-- yeah.

00:30:52.400 --> 00:30:59.780
So everyone see the Falcon
9 first stages land?

00:30:59.780 --> 00:31:04.410
That's running multiple
QPs all the time.

00:31:04.410 --> 00:31:10.670
One is running at 1,000 times
a second and it does not fail.

00:31:10.670 --> 00:31:13.160
So people-- and people use--

00:31:13.160 --> 00:31:18.950
I'd say just QP alone
is running probably--

00:31:18.950 --> 00:31:22.550
I would say, more than half of
all quantitative hedge funds

00:31:22.550 --> 00:31:26.600
just run QPs, period.

00:31:26.600 --> 00:31:30.590
So what that means is there
are many trillions of dollars

00:31:30.590 --> 00:31:35.250
trading right now on just
a QP that's being solved.

00:31:35.250 --> 00:31:35.780
So, OK.

00:31:35.780 --> 00:31:39.370
So I'm just saying it's
not bad to know about.

00:31:39.370 --> 00:31:39.870
So, OK.

00:31:42.390 --> 00:31:43.845
Let's look at some examples.

00:31:43.845 --> 00:31:44.970
Here's a really simple one.

00:31:44.970 --> 00:31:47.370
Without constraints,
you would get things

00:31:47.370 --> 00:31:49.080
like just least squares.

00:31:49.080 --> 00:31:51.690
So yeah, and we know
how to solve that,

00:31:51.690 --> 00:31:53.160
and that's just linear algebra.

00:31:53.160 --> 00:31:56.820
But here's what's cool, is you
can add linear constraints now.

00:31:56.820 --> 00:31:59.873
So-- I mean-- and this is--

00:31:59.873 --> 00:32:02.040
I mean, you can add linear
constraints, and that's--

00:32:02.040 --> 00:32:05.010
and actually, if you can
add linear constraints,

00:32:05.010 --> 00:32:08.010
but the critical part
of that is to say,

00:32:08.010 --> 00:32:10.200
if you add linear
constraints, the problem

00:32:10.200 --> 00:32:13.608
is still entirely tractable.

00:32:13.608 --> 00:32:15.150
And I guarantee you,
there are fields

00:32:15.150 --> 00:32:18.150
where they do not know that.

00:32:18.150 --> 00:32:20.390
Every year, people--

00:32:20.390 --> 00:32:22.710
I have people say
like, oh, yeah, we're

00:32:22.710 --> 00:32:24.210
fitting these
parameters and we do

00:32:24.210 --> 00:32:25.890
these very complicated
experiments

00:32:25.890 --> 00:32:29.277
or long computations,
but the parameters like--

00:32:29.277 --> 00:32:31.110
some of the parameters
have to be in ranges.

00:32:31.110 --> 00:32:33.402
Like, they have-- some of
them have to be non-negative.

00:32:33.402 --> 00:32:36.150
And then they'll invent some
unbelievably complicated

00:32:36.150 --> 00:32:38.790
terrible method for like
iteratively doing stuff,

00:32:38.790 --> 00:32:42.820
and you're like, the right
answer is, dude, that's a QP.

00:32:42.820 --> 00:32:44.170
Like, just solve it.

00:32:44.170 --> 00:32:45.610
Like just, be quiet.

00:32:45.610 --> 00:32:48.340
Like, it's done kind of thing.

00:32:48.340 --> 00:32:50.850
OK, so yeah.

00:32:50.850 --> 00:32:52.600
I mean-- and there's
lots of interesting--

00:32:52.600 --> 00:32:54.558
I mean, even just this
one, there's whole-- oh,

00:32:54.558 --> 00:32:57.040
so here's a fun one.

00:32:57.040 --> 00:33:00.400
To do least squares
where I insist--

00:33:00.400 --> 00:33:02.650
I'm going to insist
that the entries of x

00:33:02.650 --> 00:33:04.780
are non-decreasing.

00:33:04.780 --> 00:33:05.650
Everybody got that?

00:33:05.650 --> 00:33:09.190
So x1 is less than or equal to
x2 is less than or equal to x3

00:33:09.190 --> 00:33:09.700
and so on.

00:33:09.700 --> 00:33:12.550
That, by the way, would be
a really good model of wear

00:33:12.550 --> 00:33:14.750
and tear and damage
in the system.

00:33:14.750 --> 00:33:17.830
So if a jet engine
is running, then

00:33:17.830 --> 00:33:22.210
we'll just assume that if I
pull it apart and go in there

00:33:22.210 --> 00:33:25.000
and start measuring all
my critical clearances,

00:33:25.000 --> 00:33:28.210
I'm not going to-- after it runs
another 10,000 hours of flight,

00:33:28.210 --> 00:33:30.220
I'm not going to find
that it's in better shape

00:33:30.220 --> 00:33:33.372
than it was before.

00:33:33.372 --> 00:33:35.080
So that would be-- so
that's, by the way,

00:33:35.080 --> 00:33:36.288
a whole field and statistics.

00:33:36.288 --> 00:33:38.470
I think it's called isotonic
regression or something

00:33:38.470 --> 00:33:38.970
like that.

00:33:38.970 --> 00:33:42.970
There's whole books written on
it, which I find humorous right

00:33:42.970 --> 00:33:46.690
because here's how we do it.

00:33:46.690 --> 00:33:48.590
So, I mean, for us,
it's just really simple.

00:33:48.590 --> 00:33:50.410
It's like, excuse me?

00:33:50.410 --> 00:33:51.430
It's a QP.

00:33:51.430 --> 00:33:53.830
Like, that's what it is.

00:33:53.830 --> 00:33:56.890
And so what it means
is you should not

00:33:56.890 --> 00:33:59.390
and you do not need to
talk about how to solve it.

00:33:59.390 --> 00:34:01.390
I mean, you can have an
intelligent conversation

00:34:01.390 --> 00:34:04.120
about it if you want to
do it at super big scale,

00:34:04.120 --> 00:34:06.730
if you want to do
it and embed it

00:34:06.730 --> 00:34:09.639
in some safety-critical
thing so that its failure

00:34:09.639 --> 00:34:11.920
rate has to be zero.

00:34:11.920 --> 00:34:13.690
Then we can have a
sensible conversation.

00:34:13.690 --> 00:34:15.670
But you can't-- other
than that, it's silly.

00:34:15.670 --> 00:34:18.159
That's, for you,
starting next week,

00:34:18.159 --> 00:34:20.679
it's, I believe,
one line of Python.

00:34:20.679 --> 00:34:21.310
Two.

00:34:21.310 --> 00:34:21.969
Fine.

00:34:21.969 --> 00:34:23.080
Two.

00:34:23.080 --> 00:34:24.400
So it's nothing.

00:34:24.400 --> 00:34:25.210
OK.

00:34:25.210 --> 00:34:27.100
Everybody got this?

00:34:27.100 --> 00:34:29.590
So-- I mean, it's already--
these are already-- like these

00:34:29.590 --> 00:34:31.370
are useful things right here.

00:34:31.370 --> 00:34:33.940
So here's one.

00:34:33.940 --> 00:34:35.949
Let's do linear program
with random cost.

00:34:35.949 --> 00:34:37.239
And this is actually the--

00:34:37.239 --> 00:34:38.980
it's actually kind
of interesting.

00:34:38.980 --> 00:34:42.260
So that's a huge area.

00:34:42.260 --> 00:34:44.530
It's called robustness.

00:34:44.530 --> 00:34:47.889
And what it says is,
it acknowledges--

00:34:47.889 --> 00:34:49.570
so here's a-- OK,
let's step back

00:34:49.570 --> 00:34:52.090
and I'll give you the big
picture on robustness.

00:34:52.090 --> 00:34:57.310
When people use
optimization, you're

00:34:57.310 --> 00:35:00.400
assuming your constraints
and your objective and all

00:35:00.400 --> 00:35:02.957
that, they're like,
quote, "accurate" to eight

00:35:02.957 --> 00:35:03.790
significant figures.

00:35:03.790 --> 00:35:05.047
That's what you're doing.

00:35:05.047 --> 00:35:07.630
And you're modeling everything
by floats and things like that.

00:35:07.630 --> 00:35:13.690
So it's not-- it shouldn't take
much-- if you've done anything

00:35:13.690 --> 00:35:15.310
in engineering or
anything actually

00:35:15.310 --> 00:35:19.420
in any kind of real field, you
would know that of course they

00:35:19.420 --> 00:35:22.090
don't know the coefficients.

00:35:22.090 --> 00:35:25.690
Like in our diet
problem, every number--

00:35:25.690 --> 00:35:29.080
like the costs, it's
because you ask somebody

00:35:29.080 --> 00:35:30.880
or you check some
market or something,

00:35:30.880 --> 00:35:33.130
but maybe the costs have
changed in the last two days.

00:35:33.130 --> 00:35:34.580
You know what I'm saying.

00:35:34.580 --> 00:35:37.410
If I have a model of
a mechanical system,

00:35:37.410 --> 00:35:39.260
maybe I'm manufacturing
it really well,

00:35:39.260 --> 00:35:40.700
but you don't know the--

00:35:40.700 --> 00:35:43.242
you don't know the third digit
of the Young's modulus, that's

00:35:43.242 --> 00:35:44.570
ridiculous.

00:35:44.570 --> 00:35:46.250
It's absurd, in fact.

00:35:46.250 --> 00:35:50.930
In finance, I guess my friends
say about their models,

00:35:50.930 --> 00:35:55.520
they said, we're happy
if we get the sign right.

00:35:55.520 --> 00:35:58.580
Strongly suggesting that
everyone knows the third digit

00:35:58.580 --> 00:36:00.920
is like just a complete joke.

00:36:00.920 --> 00:36:03.740
The second digit is actually
kind of a joke, too.

00:36:03.740 --> 00:36:06.380
So they're worried
about the leading sign.

00:36:06.380 --> 00:36:07.520
So, OK.

00:36:07.520 --> 00:36:10.050
Anyway, so there's lots of
ways that people handle this,

00:36:10.050 --> 00:36:12.080
and we're going to come back and
talk about this in great detail

00:36:12.080 --> 00:36:12.580
later.

00:36:12.580 --> 00:36:14.840
It's actually the entirety
of like regularization.

00:36:14.840 --> 00:36:17.670
It comes up in statistics
and model-fitting there.

00:36:17.670 --> 00:36:20.090
It comes up everywhere.

00:36:20.090 --> 00:36:22.625
OK, so this will be our
first foray into it.

00:36:22.625 --> 00:36:24.500
So what we're going to
do is we're just going

00:36:24.500 --> 00:36:27.690
to say that the cost is random.

00:36:27.690 --> 00:36:30.560
So when you-- so the
way-- this problem

00:36:30.560 --> 00:36:32.340
is going to go like this.

00:36:32.340 --> 00:36:36.220
It says, when you choose an
x, we don't even speak to you

00:36:36.220 --> 00:36:37.940
if x is not feasible.

00:36:37.940 --> 00:36:40.120
So it has to satisfy Ax
equals b, Gx less than h.

00:36:40.120 --> 00:36:43.000
So let's say I construct a diet.

00:36:43.000 --> 00:36:45.070
And then the problem
is now the cost--

00:36:45.070 --> 00:36:46.778
I'd say, how much is
it going to cost me?

00:36:46.778 --> 00:36:49.680
And they're like, I don't
know, it's a random variable.

00:36:49.680 --> 00:36:50.640
You go, oh, OK.

00:36:50.640 --> 00:36:52.140
And so you could
do things like just

00:36:52.140 --> 00:36:54.300
minimize the mean on average.

00:36:54.300 --> 00:36:56.610
I mean, if you're going to
do this every 10 seconds

00:36:56.610 --> 00:36:58.620
for like the next
year, the argument

00:36:58.620 --> 00:37:01.200
would be everything would
average out and that'd be cool.

00:37:01.200 --> 00:37:03.220
But you might also care about--

00:37:03.220 --> 00:37:05.190
for example, the variance.

00:37:05.190 --> 00:37:07.080
And by the way, this
can get way fancier

00:37:07.080 --> 00:37:08.500
and we'll do that later.

00:37:08.500 --> 00:37:10.770
So here, this is a
very traditional thing,

00:37:10.770 --> 00:37:12.960
is this is-- that's the cost.

00:37:12.960 --> 00:37:14.700
That's the mean cost.

00:37:14.700 --> 00:37:17.380
And then gamma is
a positive number.

00:37:17.380 --> 00:37:19.470
That's called the risk
aversion parameter.

00:37:19.470 --> 00:37:22.680
Sigma is the covariance of c.

00:37:22.680 --> 00:37:26.460
And so this is the variance
of the cost induced

00:37:26.460 --> 00:37:28.980
by your choice of x.

00:37:28.980 --> 00:37:32.620
So that's the idea.

00:37:32.620 --> 00:37:37.150
And then this people would
call the risk-adjusted cost.

00:37:37.150 --> 00:37:39.550
That's what that
would be called.

00:37:39.550 --> 00:37:41.500
Like, say-- and you'd
probably quote it and--

00:37:41.500 --> 00:37:42.970
I mean, it's
probably-- honestly,

00:37:42.970 --> 00:37:44.200
you'd probably take this--

00:37:44.200 --> 00:37:46.630
a more sensible thing would
be to take a square root here,

00:37:46.630 --> 00:37:49.510
but this is fine because then
they're on the same units,

00:37:49.510 --> 00:37:50.470
but anyway.

00:37:50.470 --> 00:37:51.860
So that's what this is.

00:37:51.860 --> 00:37:54.490
This is called
risk-adjusted cost.

00:37:54.490 --> 00:37:57.490
Oh, and you-- in principle--

00:37:57.490 --> 00:37:59.320
so anyway, when
you do this, that's

00:37:59.320 --> 00:38:02.440
a QP right because that's a
convex quadratic function.

00:38:02.440 --> 00:38:04.870
Oh, provided gamma is positive.

00:38:04.870 --> 00:38:08.080
If gamma is negative,
that's interesting.

00:38:08.080 --> 00:38:09.490
In fact, what do--

00:38:09.490 --> 00:38:12.010
if someone's doing--
if you want to minimize

00:38:12.010 --> 00:38:14.050
a risk-adjusted cost
with a gamma negative,

00:38:14.050 --> 00:38:16.960
that's called risk-seeking.

00:38:16.960 --> 00:38:21.490
And-- OK, just a couple
of things about solving

00:38:21.490 --> 00:38:22.720
about that problem.

00:38:22.720 --> 00:38:26.980
A, it's a really stupid thing
to do because you're saying--

00:38:26.980 --> 00:38:29.680
I mean, unless it's like some
thrill-seeker-- you can imagine

00:38:29.680 --> 00:38:33.050
something where you're like,
yeah, I'd like the same mean,

00:38:33.050 --> 00:38:35.300
but I'd like the variance
to be way bigger-- it just--

00:38:35.300 --> 00:38:37.925
it doesn't make any-- so number
one, it's a stupid thing to do.

00:38:37.925 --> 00:38:41.330
Number two, let's talk about
the computational complexity.

00:38:41.330 --> 00:38:42.860
If someone says,
no, no, no, no, no.

00:38:42.860 --> 00:38:44.395
No, you don't
understand, I really--

00:38:44.395 --> 00:38:46.520
I'm designing-- I'm doing
some entertainment thing,

00:38:46.520 --> 00:38:47.882
it has to-- it has to be--

00:38:47.882 --> 00:38:49.340
it has to be some
thrills in there,

00:38:49.340 --> 00:38:51.950
so I want gamma to be minus 0.1.

00:38:51.950 --> 00:38:52.940
Is that a QP?

00:38:57.860 --> 00:38:58.430
No.

00:38:58.430 --> 00:38:59.540
It's not.

00:38:59.540 --> 00:39:03.740
Because this becomes no
longer-- it's no longer convex.

00:39:03.740 --> 00:39:04.580
So anyway.

00:39:04.580 --> 00:39:07.280
So this actually
occurs a lot where

00:39:07.280 --> 00:39:12.043
you have something where there's
a box with the things-- these

00:39:12.043 --> 00:39:13.710
are the problems that
are easy to solve.

00:39:13.710 --> 00:39:15.300
These are the problems
that are hard to solve,

00:39:15.300 --> 00:39:17.390
these are the problems
that are sensible to solve,

00:39:17.390 --> 00:39:19.848
and these are the problems that
are really stupid to solve.

00:39:19.848 --> 00:39:23.480
And it doesn't split up
25-25-25-25 all the time.

00:39:23.480 --> 00:39:25.520
I mean, it's actually quite--

00:39:25.520 --> 00:39:30.050
it's very usual that the
problem you should be solving

00:39:30.050 --> 00:39:31.820
is actually the one
that's solvable.

00:39:31.820 --> 00:39:34.810
And then the other problem--

00:39:34.810 --> 00:39:37.310
the other types of problems are
ones that, A, you should not

00:39:37.310 --> 00:39:39.080
be solving; and B,
that's just fine

00:39:39.080 --> 00:39:40.610
because you can't do it anyway.

00:39:40.610 --> 00:39:43.620
So anyway, it's just weird and
that's just how it works out.

00:39:43.620 --> 00:39:44.120
OK.

00:39:47.180 --> 00:39:48.680
Oh, this leads us
to another one.

00:39:48.680 --> 00:39:50.680
Yeah, you don't hear so
much about this anymore,

00:39:50.680 --> 00:39:54.070
but you still do, is QCQP.

00:39:54.070 --> 00:39:56.710
And so this is
where, wow, instead

00:39:56.710 --> 00:40:01.090
of having linear inequalities,
you now have convex quadratic.

00:40:01.090 --> 00:40:03.580
So this is-- that's
a so-called QCQP.

00:40:03.580 --> 00:40:06.400
And you'll still hear some
people talk about that.

00:40:06.400 --> 00:40:07.420
OK.

00:40:07.420 --> 00:40:10.330
Now we get to something
that's kind of modern,

00:40:10.330 --> 00:40:13.600
meaning it's from
the last 25 years.

00:40:13.600 --> 00:40:18.580
And it looks just like QCQP
or something like that,

00:40:18.580 --> 00:40:20.290
but it's not.

00:40:20.290 --> 00:40:22.360
And it's also like
an incredible--

00:40:22.360 --> 00:40:24.160
it's an innocent-looking
problem.

00:40:24.160 --> 00:40:27.520
It's truly unbelievable
what problems

00:40:27.520 --> 00:40:29.290
can be reduced to this.

00:40:29.290 --> 00:40:31.060
So let's take a look at it.

00:40:31.060 --> 00:40:33.520
It's, you minimize
a linear function.

00:40:33.520 --> 00:40:34.210
That's cool.

00:40:34.210 --> 00:40:35.620
That's like linear programming.

00:40:35.620 --> 00:40:38.980
You have linear
equality constraints.

00:40:38.980 --> 00:40:40.750
And then you have a
bunch of constraints

00:40:40.750 --> 00:40:42.930
that-- by the way, if this
was just-- if I made A

00:40:42.930 --> 00:40:46.210
and b 0, this would be 0,
this would be a general LP.

00:40:46.210 --> 00:40:49.840
So what that says is whatever
second-order cone programming

00:40:49.840 --> 00:40:53.830
is, it generalizes LP
for sure because any LP I

00:40:53.830 --> 00:40:55.960
can solve with SOCP.

00:40:55.960 --> 00:40:57.680
And it just looks like this.

00:40:57.680 --> 00:40:59.410
And so it's a 2-norm.

00:40:59.410 --> 00:41:04.390
By the way, this is 2-norm
and it is not 2-norm squared.

00:41:04.390 --> 00:41:05.890
So that's one of
the things you have

00:41:05.890 --> 00:41:10.790
to fight that because of
our collective training

00:41:10.790 --> 00:41:15.040
and hundreds of years of
math before us, whenever you

00:41:15.040 --> 00:41:18.950
see a 2-norm, you square it.

00:41:18.950 --> 00:41:20.190
We all know this.

00:41:20.190 --> 00:41:21.110
Everybody does that.

00:41:21.110 --> 00:41:21.740
And you don't--

00:41:21.740 --> 00:41:24.260
And there might be
some good reasons.

00:41:24.260 --> 00:41:26.397
Like now you get this
analytical solution and,

00:41:26.397 --> 00:41:27.230
I don't know, this--

00:41:27.230 --> 00:41:29.690
so now it's differentiable
and I don't know.

00:41:29.690 --> 00:41:32.900
But mostly it's because
it's historical.

00:41:32.900 --> 00:41:35.070
That's really why it
is, why you square it.

00:41:35.070 --> 00:41:36.693
So anyway, very important point.

00:41:36.693 --> 00:41:37.360
This is squared.

00:41:40.670 --> 00:41:41.240
OK.

00:41:41.240 --> 00:41:43.190
So that's a second-order
cone program.

00:41:43.190 --> 00:41:45.140
And why?

00:41:45.140 --> 00:41:46.880
Because basically
this constraint

00:41:46.880 --> 00:41:52.670
here says that the image
under an affine mapping

00:41:52.670 --> 00:41:55.430
is in the second-order cone.

00:41:55.430 --> 00:41:56.660
That's what it says.

00:41:56.660 --> 00:41:59.240
Because the second-order cone
is all things of the form

00:41:59.240 --> 00:42:04.260
2-norm of u is less than v.
Use a vector V as a scalar,

00:42:04.260 --> 00:42:06.270
and that's exactly what this is.

00:42:06.270 --> 00:42:08.180
This is the 2-norm of
that is less than that,

00:42:08.180 --> 00:42:09.650
and that's exactly what this is.

00:42:09.650 --> 00:42:11.030
So that's it.

00:42:11.030 --> 00:42:13.640
And this includes QCQP, LP.

00:42:13.640 --> 00:42:17.240
Actually, weird
secret, it actually

00:42:17.240 --> 00:42:20.420
includes probably
95% of any problem

00:42:20.420 --> 00:42:23.990
you would ever want to solve
from 100 different fields.

00:42:23.990 --> 00:42:28.160
So you will start using CVXPY.

00:42:28.160 --> 00:42:30.680
You will write down
weird functions

00:42:30.680 --> 00:42:33.830
like quadratic over lin and
all sorts of other crazy stuff.

00:42:33.830 --> 00:42:38.080
Really complicated geometric
means all sorts of weird stuff.

00:42:38.080 --> 00:42:42.610
They will be reduced
to an SOCP and solve.

00:42:42.610 --> 00:42:44.800
So this is the--

00:42:44.800 --> 00:42:46.330
yeah, this is the idea.

00:42:46.330 --> 00:42:49.600
And this is-- like I said,
this is relatively new.

00:42:49.600 --> 00:42:55.450
It's also, I think for a
broad group of problems,

00:42:55.450 --> 00:42:57.790
it's almost like--

00:42:57.790 --> 00:43:01.270
this is the low-level language.

00:43:01.270 --> 00:43:04.190
This is like the 3-byte
code of convex optimization.

00:43:04.190 --> 00:43:04.690
Roughly.

00:43:04.690 --> 00:43:06.490
For a reasonable fraction of it.

00:43:06.490 --> 00:43:09.580
That what it is you write
a problem a high form--

00:43:09.580 --> 00:43:11.440
in a high-level form,
it gets compiled

00:43:11.440 --> 00:43:14.010
into a second-order cone
program, and then it gets

00:43:14.010 --> 00:43:14.510
solved.

00:43:14.510 --> 00:43:16.240
And what's nice about
that arrangement is it

00:43:16.240 --> 00:43:18.157
means that people all
over the world right now

00:43:18.157 --> 00:43:22.540
are working on solvers for
second-order cone programs.

00:43:22.540 --> 00:43:23.320
Why?

00:43:23.320 --> 00:43:26.560
Because a much larger
group of people

00:43:26.560 --> 00:43:29.230
is actually solving problems
that they don't even know

00:43:29.230 --> 00:43:31.600
can be reduced to
second-order cone programs,

00:43:31.600 --> 00:43:34.660
but then-- so it all
works out very well.

00:43:34.660 --> 00:43:38.060
So, OK.

00:43:38.060 --> 00:43:39.260
Let's do an example.

00:43:39.260 --> 00:43:42.080
We'll do a robust
linear programming.

00:43:42.080 --> 00:43:44.520
And here's the way
it's going to work.

00:43:44.520 --> 00:43:49.317
So this time, before we said
the objective is uncertain, now

00:43:49.317 --> 00:43:50.900
we're going to now
we're going to make

00:43:50.900 --> 00:43:54.350
the inequalities uncertain.

00:43:54.350 --> 00:43:58.250
And what we're going
to do is we will--

00:43:58.250 --> 00:43:59.850
we'll look at what happens--

00:43:59.850 --> 00:44:03.320
we're going to look at just what
happens when ai is uncertain.

00:44:03.320 --> 00:44:07.910
And look in our diet
problem, that says, oh,

00:44:07.910 --> 00:44:13.117
how many nutrients do you get
per gram of this foodstuff?

00:44:13.117 --> 00:44:14.450
And the answer is, I don't know.

00:44:14.450 --> 00:44:15.680
Depends on the shipment.

00:44:15.680 --> 00:44:19.460
Like we yanked--
actually, in June,

00:44:19.460 --> 00:44:21.550
they're slightly
different-- and whatever.

00:44:21.550 --> 00:44:24.050
And by the way, Friday's batch
is different from Thursday's.

00:44:24.050 --> 00:44:26.900
I mean, not grossly, but
they'll be-- it could be-- it

00:44:26.900 --> 00:44:29.300
could easily be considerable.

00:44:29.300 --> 00:44:30.980
So anyway, so you do that.

00:44:30.980 --> 00:44:34.070
By the way, then you fit
an ellipsoid to those.

00:44:34.070 --> 00:44:35.750
And then what you're
doing is you're

00:44:35.750 --> 00:44:38.460
saying, please
construct me a diet that

00:44:38.460 --> 00:44:41.730
satisfies these minimum
health requirements.

00:44:41.730 --> 00:44:44.190
And you'd say, oh, but
I want this diet-- that

00:44:44.190 --> 00:44:46.260
should work no matter
what gets delivered

00:44:46.260 --> 00:44:50.940
to us with different amounts
of nutrients per foodstuff.

00:44:50.940 --> 00:44:52.410
Everybody following this?

00:44:52.410 --> 00:44:54.930
That's the robust
solution to that.

00:44:54.930 --> 00:44:55.440
So, OK.

00:44:55.440 --> 00:44:58.270
So that looks like this.

00:44:58.270 --> 00:44:59.890
OK.

00:44:59.890 --> 00:45:02.290
And another one is
the stochastic model.

00:45:02.290 --> 00:45:04.750
And the stochastic model,
you'd say, oh yeah,

00:45:04.750 --> 00:45:06.730
you know what the?
ai's are random.

00:45:06.730 --> 00:45:09.043
They're just they're
Gaussian or whatever.

00:45:09.043 --> 00:45:10.210
They have some distribution.

00:45:10.210 --> 00:45:12.670
And you'd say, yeah,
I want the probability

00:45:12.670 --> 00:45:16.240
that the constraint holds to
be greater than some number.

00:45:16.240 --> 00:45:19.540
Typical numbers of
eta would be 0.9.

00:45:19.540 --> 00:45:20.830
That's pretty loose.

00:45:20.830 --> 00:45:23.650
0.95, 0.99.

00:45:23.650 --> 00:45:27.730
It could be 0.99999 or
something like that,

00:45:27.730 --> 00:45:31.090
but and that's OK as long as
you understand what you're doing

00:45:31.090 --> 00:45:33.610
when you solve that problem.

00:45:33.610 --> 00:45:37.090
Yeah, if someone said, yeah,
no, no, no, we do-- we--

00:45:37.090 --> 00:45:40.111
make sure it works for--

00:45:40.111 --> 00:45:43.150
that the probability
is bigger than 99.9%.

00:45:43.150 --> 00:45:46.690
Of course, the question there
is, there's no one in any field

00:45:46.690 --> 00:45:48.640
that I'm aware of
who actually knows

00:45:48.640 --> 00:45:51.850
the distribution of something--
the tail of their distribution

00:45:51.850 --> 00:45:52.548
that well.

00:45:52.548 --> 00:45:54.340
This is-- sorry, this
is just a weird rant,

00:45:54.340 --> 00:45:56.140
but I'm just I'm
doing it anyway.

00:45:56.140 --> 00:45:58.190
Like, there's no such thing.

00:45:58.190 --> 00:46:02.720
If you go anywhere and
someone says, oh, we

00:46:02.720 --> 00:46:05.390
want we want the probability
to be more than 99.9%,

00:46:05.390 --> 00:46:07.310
it's hilarious to me.

00:46:07.310 --> 00:46:09.530
It's just ridiculous.

00:46:09.530 --> 00:46:11.840
OK, so OK.

00:46:11.840 --> 00:46:14.300
However, it's actually still
a perfectly good problem

00:46:14.300 --> 00:46:16.880
to solve because when
someone looks at your code

00:46:16.880 --> 00:46:19.400
and it says the probability
should be bigger than 99.9%,

00:46:19.400 --> 00:46:22.490
you're like, wow,
you must really

00:46:22.490 --> 00:46:25.100
have a good handle on the
tails of that distribution.

00:46:25.100 --> 00:46:26.780
And then here's
the correct answer.

00:46:26.780 --> 00:46:28.970
You go, oh, yeah, no, we have--

00:46:28.970 --> 00:46:31.220
no, we have terrible models
of the tail distribution.

00:46:31.220 --> 00:46:33.260
And you go, but your
probability-- and you go,

00:46:33.260 --> 00:46:35.270
that just means like, really?

00:46:35.270 --> 00:46:36.885
Please I want this to hold.

00:46:36.885 --> 00:46:37.760
That's what it means.

00:46:37.760 --> 00:46:39.600
It doesn't mean
anything more than that.

00:46:39.600 --> 00:46:40.670
So, OK.

00:46:40.670 --> 00:46:41.630
Everybody got that?

00:46:41.630 --> 00:46:42.740
So-- well, that was weird.

00:46:42.740 --> 00:46:43.700
That was a weird rant.

00:46:43.700 --> 00:46:44.200
OK.

00:46:44.200 --> 00:46:46.610
So, fine.

00:46:46.610 --> 00:46:49.550
OK, so let's do the
deterministic one.

00:46:49.550 --> 00:46:51.170
That's going to
turn into an SOCP.

00:46:51.170 --> 00:46:52.350
How do you do that?

00:46:52.350 --> 00:46:59.830
Well, we write the ellipsoids as
a center plus, here, a matrix--

00:46:59.830 --> 00:47:02.320
a linear mapping
of the unit ball.

00:47:02.320 --> 00:47:05.980
So that's my-- so p tells
me like the ellipsoid--

00:47:05.980 --> 00:47:08.860
where is it-- which axis is
it big in and stuff like that.

00:47:08.860 --> 00:47:12.160
So that-- by the way, that's
one of several representation

00:47:12.160 --> 00:47:14.800
of ellipsoid, that's what
we're going to use here.

00:47:14.800 --> 00:47:16.420
So we do this.

00:47:16.420 --> 00:47:19.780
And here, if you want
to know, we really

00:47:19.780 --> 00:47:23.770
have to work out, what is the
maximum of this expression

00:47:23.770 --> 00:47:24.880
over that ellipsoid?

00:47:24.880 --> 00:47:27.820
And then you just
end up with this.

00:47:27.820 --> 00:47:29.770
And now, again, we very--

00:47:29.770 --> 00:47:31.700
we sit down.

00:47:31.700 --> 00:47:33.700
We actually, with
Chebyshev center,

00:47:33.700 --> 00:47:37.240
we encountered something with a
2-norm, but the argument of it

00:47:37.240 --> 00:47:39.830
was data, and therefore,
it was a constant

00:47:39.830 --> 00:47:42.700
and it was just a red flag,
and your yellow flashing light

00:47:42.700 --> 00:47:44.260
in your brain didn't matter.

00:47:44.260 --> 00:47:46.045
Here, though, we
have to be careful.

00:47:46.045 --> 00:47:47.170
So you look at the problem.

00:47:47.170 --> 00:47:49.060
That's linear, that's cool.

00:47:49.060 --> 00:47:54.413
And this objective is indeed
a second-order cone problem--

00:47:54.413 --> 00:47:55.705
a second-order cone constraint.

00:47:55.705 --> 00:47:59.900
People would say an
SOCP constraint here.

00:47:59.900 --> 00:48:01.610
Actually, it's
super interesting.

00:48:01.610 --> 00:48:03.718
Like if you just
said, you know what?

00:48:03.718 --> 00:48:05.510
I don't care about
those uncertainty stuff,

00:48:05.510 --> 00:48:06.343
I don't believe it.

00:48:06.343 --> 00:48:08.510
Besides, this stuff is only
off by plus or minus 5%,

00:48:08.510 --> 00:48:09.320
I don't care.

00:48:09.320 --> 00:48:13.760
Then what you do is you just
replace the a with ai bar,

00:48:13.760 --> 00:48:17.490
which is the middle value.

00:48:17.490 --> 00:48:18.390
So you just do that.

00:48:18.390 --> 00:48:22.140
If you do that, you
get this problem here.

00:48:22.140 --> 00:48:23.760
That's an LP.

00:48:23.760 --> 00:48:25.380
But then you'd say--

00:48:25.380 --> 00:48:27.820
you'd say, yeah, but
there's uncertainty,

00:48:27.820 --> 00:48:32.760
so I would like a little bit
of margin in my inequality just

00:48:32.760 --> 00:48:36.350
to cover me when
a wiggles around.

00:48:36.350 --> 00:48:38.160
What's interesting
is this tells you

00:48:38.160 --> 00:48:41.550
exactly how much margin to use.

00:48:41.550 --> 00:48:45.570
So what this says is,
wherever p is big,

00:48:45.570 --> 00:48:48.650
should avoid having
x in that direction.

00:48:48.650 --> 00:48:49.350
Makes sense.

00:48:49.350 --> 00:48:51.890
In fact, you could even--
you could literally

00:48:51.890 --> 00:48:56.780
flag that number and call
that the-- that is the margin,

00:48:56.780 --> 00:48:58.790
and the cool part is,
it's a function of x.

00:48:58.790 --> 00:49:00.960
It's a function of your choice.

00:49:00.960 --> 00:49:02.850
Everybody got this?

00:49:02.850 --> 00:49:08.270
So-- OK, so that's an example.

00:49:08.270 --> 00:49:13.410
So a robust LP with
ellipsoidal uncertainty

00:49:13.410 --> 00:49:14.670
is, in fact, an SOCP.

00:49:17.270 --> 00:49:18.560
OK.

00:49:18.560 --> 00:49:21.470
We can also do the same thing
with a statistical model

00:49:21.470 --> 00:49:23.120
and it works just fine.

00:49:23.120 --> 00:49:25.280
Weirdly, you also
end up with an SOCP.

00:49:25.280 --> 00:49:27.198
So here, here I say,
no, you know what?

00:49:27.198 --> 00:49:29.240
I don't know, I took enough
statistics and stuff.

00:49:29.240 --> 00:49:31.070
So ai's Gaussian
with a mean a bar,

00:49:31.070 --> 00:49:33.920
that's a vector, and
a covariance matrix.

00:49:33.920 --> 00:49:35.870
So that means that
this linear function

00:49:35.870 --> 00:49:38.420
of it, that's also Gaussian,
because linear function

00:49:38.420 --> 00:49:40.700
of a Gaussian
vector is Gaussian.

00:49:40.700 --> 00:49:44.900
And it's got a mean a bar
transpose x and it's got

00:49:44.900 --> 00:49:46.550
a variance x transpose sigma x.

00:49:46.550 --> 00:49:48.710
And that means the
probability that--

00:49:48.710 --> 00:49:51.530
it means that this is just a--
that's just a Gaussian-- so

00:49:51.530 --> 00:49:52.310
a scalar Gaussian.

00:49:52.310 --> 00:49:53.685
The probability
a scalar Gaussian

00:49:53.685 --> 00:49:55.700
is less than a number
is equal to this.

00:49:55.700 --> 00:49:58.340
You subtract the
mean and then you

00:49:58.340 --> 00:50:00.090
divide by the
standard deviation,

00:50:00.090 --> 00:50:05.090
you get an n01 variable, and
then you look at the CDF.

00:50:05.090 --> 00:50:08.510
So that's the CDF of a Gaussian.

00:50:08.510 --> 00:50:11.030
Everybody following this?

00:50:11.030 --> 00:50:11.540
Yeah.

00:50:11.540 --> 00:50:13.910
By the way, it's not
looking super good

00:50:13.910 --> 00:50:18.355
here because these
are replaced with--

00:50:18.355 --> 00:50:19.980
now you do know some
things about that.

00:50:19.980 --> 00:50:23.220
For example, that's a log
concave function, the Gaussian

00:50:23.220 --> 00:50:25.530
CDF.

00:50:25.530 --> 00:50:27.630
But doesn't sound
like this is going

00:50:27.630 --> 00:50:30.150
to end up as a
second-order cone program

00:50:30.150 --> 00:50:32.550
because it just
doesn't sound quadratic

00:50:32.550 --> 00:50:33.675
or something like that.

00:50:33.675 --> 00:50:34.930
But It is.

00:50:34.930 --> 00:50:40.248
And the reason is
pretty straightforward.

00:50:40.248 --> 00:50:42.540
What we want to do is we want
to say that this thing is

00:50:42.540 --> 00:50:45.450
bigger than or equal to eta.

00:50:45.450 --> 00:50:47.010
Eta is 0.95.

00:50:47.010 --> 00:50:48.540
There you go.

00:50:48.540 --> 00:50:52.140
But this phi is monotone.

00:50:52.140 --> 00:50:58.890
So this says this expression is
bigger than phi inverse of eta.

00:50:58.890 --> 00:51:00.280
Everybody got that?

00:51:00.280 --> 00:51:00.780
OK.

00:51:00.780 --> 00:51:03.630
Now phi inverse
of eta, it's going

00:51:03.630 --> 00:51:08.220
to be positive because eta
is like 0.85 and stuff.

00:51:08.220 --> 00:51:12.510
Oh, actually, if eta
is less than 50%,

00:51:12.510 --> 00:51:17.280
if eta is less than 50%, phi
inverse of eta is negative.

00:51:17.280 --> 00:51:18.810
If I have a standard
Gaussian and I

00:51:18.810 --> 00:51:21.720
say, what's the inverse CDF of
0.3 I don't know what it is,

00:51:21.720 --> 00:51:24.030
but it's like minus 1/2
or something, roughly.

00:51:24.030 --> 00:51:25.950
I just made that up, but still.

00:51:25.950 --> 00:51:28.080
Everybody got this?

00:51:28.080 --> 00:51:32.730
So provided that your
required probability

00:51:32.730 --> 00:51:37.490
that the constraints
hold is more than 50%,

00:51:37.490 --> 00:51:39.020
then you can
actually multiply out

00:51:39.020 --> 00:51:40.580
this thing, this
thing bigger than

00:51:40.580 --> 00:51:43.640
or equal to phi inverse of
eta, and you multiply it out

00:51:43.640 --> 00:51:44.480
like this.

00:51:44.480 --> 00:51:46.820
And lo and behold, it's an SOCP.

00:51:46.820 --> 00:51:48.980
Oh, actually, it's
only an SOCP--

00:51:48.980 --> 00:51:52.190
if eta-- if this thing
is bigger than a 1/2.

00:51:52.190 --> 00:51:54.090
Because otherwise
this is negative,

00:51:54.090 --> 00:51:55.670
and then you got
the wrong-- you're

00:51:55.670 --> 00:51:58.010
sitting on the wrong side
of the inequality there.

00:51:58.010 --> 00:51:59.070
Everybody see that?

00:51:59.070 --> 00:52:03.540
So-- now, if someone
came up to you

00:52:03.540 --> 00:52:06.840
and said, please help me
solve this robust problem,

00:52:06.840 --> 00:52:08.140
and you go, what is it?

00:52:08.140 --> 00:52:10.473
You go, well, the thing is,
I don't know the constraints

00:52:10.473 --> 00:52:11.837
and they vary.

00:52:11.837 --> 00:52:13.920
And I'd say, well, with
what probability would you

00:52:13.920 --> 00:52:15.170
like your constraints to hold?

00:52:15.170 --> 00:52:18.120
And if they said something like,
well, 15% would be good for me,

00:52:18.120 --> 00:52:21.253
like, you would say, go away.

00:52:21.253 --> 00:52:23.670
I mean, you'd check or whatever,
but you'd send them away.

00:52:23.670 --> 00:52:26.250
And you could also say--
and as they're leaving,

00:52:26.250 --> 00:52:28.890
you can shout out and it
says, not only is that stupid,

00:52:28.890 --> 00:52:30.205
but you can't even solve it.

00:52:30.205 --> 00:52:31.830
That's what I would
say to that person.

00:52:31.830 --> 00:52:34.080
That's as they're leaving.

00:52:34.080 --> 00:52:35.140
Everybody got that?

00:52:35.140 --> 00:52:37.500
I mean, you-- there
are weird cases

00:52:37.500 --> 00:52:39.960
where you might have
risk-seeking things.

00:52:39.960 --> 00:52:41.940
The closest I've ever
come to hearing somebody

00:52:41.940 --> 00:52:47.310
describe risk-seeking something
was, OK, not socially positive,

00:52:47.310 --> 00:52:52.200
but it was somebody who was
designing missile controllers.

00:52:52.200 --> 00:52:57.240
And they said, well, if you
have high probability of making

00:52:57.240 --> 00:53:01.518
a kill, do it, but we'll take
10% because otherwise you

00:53:01.518 --> 00:53:04.060
already launched it, it's on
its way and it's not coming home

00:53:04.060 --> 00:53:05.170
and you're not going
to use it again.

00:53:05.170 --> 00:53:06.820
I don't know if
everybody got that.

00:53:06.820 --> 00:53:11.290
And I think I even quasi-accept
that as an actual application,

00:53:11.290 --> 00:53:13.690
but OK, if not a
socially positive one.

00:53:13.690 --> 00:53:14.440
But anyway.

00:53:14.440 --> 00:53:15.370
OK.

00:53:15.370 --> 00:53:17.060
That's it.

00:53:17.060 --> 00:53:18.740
OK.

00:53:18.740 --> 00:53:21.510
Next up in our tour.

00:53:21.510 --> 00:53:25.040
So next topic is
geometric programming.

00:53:25.040 --> 00:53:26.880
So you're seeing a lot of stuff.

00:53:26.880 --> 00:53:27.810
This is very cool.

00:53:27.810 --> 00:53:30.650
This goes back into
the 1940s and--

00:53:30.650 --> 00:53:33.920
actually, weirdly,
it persisted--

00:53:33.920 --> 00:53:37.230
well, let me explain what it is.

00:53:37.230 --> 00:53:40.460
So it's a class of
optimization problems

00:53:40.460 --> 00:53:43.490
where we are going to apply--

00:53:43.490 --> 00:53:46.277
it is absolutely not
a convex problem.

00:53:46.277 --> 00:53:47.360
There's no doubt about it.

00:53:47.360 --> 00:53:48.568
We'll look at it in a minute.

00:53:48.568 --> 00:53:49.580
It's very weird.

00:53:49.580 --> 00:53:51.592
But it's not a convex problem.

00:53:51.592 --> 00:53:53.050
But what we're
going to do is we're

00:53:53.050 --> 00:53:55.700
going to make a transformation.

00:53:55.700 --> 00:53:57.280
We're going to
change the variables

00:53:57.280 --> 00:53:59.110
and change a few other
things, that's all.

00:53:59.110 --> 00:54:01.150
And now it's going
to become convex

00:54:01.150 --> 00:54:02.870
under this change of variables.

00:54:02.870 --> 00:54:04.630
Now, you can solve it.

00:54:04.630 --> 00:54:08.200
It scales to gigantic problems
and all sorts of other stuff.

00:54:08.200 --> 00:54:09.757
So this is an example of that.

00:54:09.757 --> 00:54:10.840
We're going to see others.

00:54:10.840 --> 00:54:12.620
In statistics, we're
going to see them.

00:54:12.620 --> 00:54:14.860
We're going to see them
in other areas, too.

00:54:14.860 --> 00:54:18.490
So, OK.

00:54:18.490 --> 00:54:20.380
And it's got its
own weird language.

00:54:20.380 --> 00:54:20.890
Oh.

00:54:20.890 --> 00:54:22.307
The other thing I
was going to say

00:54:22.307 --> 00:54:24.610
was, this was an entire field.

00:54:24.610 --> 00:54:26.770
People talked about it,
people wrote books about it

00:54:26.770 --> 00:54:29.150
in the '60s, all sorts of stuff.

00:54:29.150 --> 00:54:32.530
It wasn't until
like around 1980s

00:54:32.530 --> 00:54:35.080
that people actually
realized it was convex.

00:54:35.080 --> 00:54:37.030
Or, sorry, it's not convex.

00:54:37.030 --> 00:54:41.320
Can be reduced to an
equivalent convex problem,

00:54:41.320 --> 00:54:43.593
and therefore, scaled.

00:54:43.593 --> 00:54:45.010
It means-- in fact,
you don't even

00:54:45.010 --> 00:54:46.468
have to say anything
at that point.

00:54:46.468 --> 00:54:49.750
You just stop and
you say, it's done.

00:54:49.750 --> 00:54:52.820
Although some of my friends
who were in Moscow in the '60s

00:54:52.820 --> 00:54:54.710
said, well, we all knew that.

00:54:54.710 --> 00:54:55.520
Like, oh.

00:54:55.520 --> 00:54:59.120
So I'm hardly surprised in the
West they didn't know that.

00:54:59.120 --> 00:55:01.670
Anyway, so everybody got this?

00:55:01.670 --> 00:55:02.210
OK.

00:55:02.210 --> 00:55:03.530
So here it is.

00:55:03.530 --> 00:55:05.150
And it's got its
own secret language,

00:55:05.150 --> 00:55:10.160
which you should never use these
words in the wrong situation

00:55:10.160 --> 00:55:13.010
because you will
sound like an idiot.

00:55:13.010 --> 00:55:15.310
Unless other people know
the background and things

00:55:15.310 --> 00:55:15.810
like that.

00:55:15.810 --> 00:55:17.850
So here's what it is.

00:55:17.850 --> 00:55:19.650
If I have a product--

00:55:19.650 --> 00:55:21.710
these are positive
variables, and I raise them

00:55:21.710 --> 00:55:24.860
to various powers-- but the
powers are just real numbers.

00:55:24.860 --> 00:55:30.080
They can be minus 1.3,
plus 2.6, minus 3.5.

00:55:30.080 --> 00:55:30.875
Everybody got that?

00:55:33.410 --> 00:55:34.360
So it's a product.

00:55:34.360 --> 00:55:36.860
By the way, I think, people
talk about this as a scaling law

00:55:36.860 --> 00:55:38.750
in a lot of engineering fields.

00:55:38.750 --> 00:55:40.995
Like, how does this go
with like Reynolds number

00:55:40.995 --> 00:55:42.870
and all that kind of--
so it's a scaling law.

00:55:42.870 --> 00:55:46.160
So I think some people
are familiar with things

00:55:46.160 --> 00:55:47.750
that look like that.

00:55:47.750 --> 00:55:49.370
So anyway.

00:55:49.370 --> 00:55:53.290
So in this field of
geometric programming,

00:55:53.290 --> 00:55:56.230
they decided to call
this a monomial.

00:55:56.230 --> 00:55:58.630
However, unfortunately,
there is a definition

00:55:58.630 --> 00:56:02.770
of monomial in math, which
has been used since like 1820

00:56:02.770 --> 00:56:06.010
or something like that, and
it is absolutely standard.

00:56:06.010 --> 00:56:06.820
It's like min.

00:56:06.820 --> 00:56:09.250
There is no doubt
what a monomial is.

00:56:09.250 --> 00:56:12.130
A monomial is one
and only one thing.

00:56:12.130 --> 00:56:18.560
It is a product of variables
with integer exponents,

00:56:18.560 --> 00:56:20.990
and the coefficient
in front can be

00:56:20.990 --> 00:56:22.730
anything, positive or negative.

00:56:22.730 --> 00:56:23.810
Everybody got that?

00:56:23.810 --> 00:56:29.630
So minus 3x1 cubed times
x squared times x5,

00:56:29.630 --> 00:56:33.880
that is-- that's a monomial that
you can say outside this room.

00:56:33.880 --> 00:56:36.160
But only in the context
of geometric programming

00:56:36.160 --> 00:56:37.660
would you call this a monomial.

00:56:37.660 --> 00:56:40.090
So just a heads
up, because if you

00:56:40.090 --> 00:56:42.160
say-- if you use-- you
know that's a monomial.

00:56:42.160 --> 00:56:43.510
If there's anybody
around who doesn't

00:56:43.510 --> 00:56:44.885
know about geometric
programming,

00:56:44.885 --> 00:56:46.510
they'll think you're an idiot.

00:56:46.510 --> 00:56:47.530
So, OK.

00:56:47.530 --> 00:56:49.510
And this is even worse.

00:56:49.510 --> 00:56:51.610
A posynomial.

00:56:51.610 --> 00:56:53.600
And I'm sorry, we
didn't make this up.

00:56:53.600 --> 00:56:57.820
This is simply the name
assigned to it in the 1960s.

00:56:57.820 --> 00:57:01.300
It's a sum of monomials.

00:57:01.300 --> 00:57:02.980
That's called a--
and I think it's

00:57:02.980 --> 00:57:05.770
supposed to be
positive and monomial,

00:57:05.770 --> 00:57:08.860
forget the fact that
their use of monomial

00:57:08.860 --> 00:57:11.800
is not the absolutely
standard use.

00:57:11.800 --> 00:57:13.450
So anyway, whatever.

00:57:13.450 --> 00:57:16.210
That's posynomial.

00:57:16.210 --> 00:57:18.320
And then a geometric
program looks like this.

00:57:18.320 --> 00:57:19.870
It says you have
positive variables.

00:57:19.870 --> 00:57:21.520
That's implicit.

00:57:21.520 --> 00:57:26.050
And you minimize a
posynomial subject to--

00:57:26.050 --> 00:57:28.420
and don't say
posynomial-- make--

00:57:28.420 --> 00:57:31.270
if you say that, make
sure everybody around you

00:57:31.270 --> 00:57:35.560
within any hearing
distance knows about GP

00:57:35.560 --> 00:57:38.170
and knows that
you know about GP,

00:57:38.170 --> 00:57:41.628
otherwise don't say
that word because--

00:57:41.628 --> 00:57:42.670
I mean, you could try it.

00:57:42.670 --> 00:57:44.212
Just walk over to
the Math Department

00:57:44.212 --> 00:57:45.110
and see what happens.

00:57:45.110 --> 00:57:46.960
But anyway.

00:57:46.960 --> 00:57:47.500
OK.

00:57:47.500 --> 00:57:49.750
And then-- and you have
equality constraints.

00:57:49.750 --> 00:57:53.140
So this is not remotely convex.

00:57:53.140 --> 00:57:55.750
I mean, these are highly--

00:57:55.750 --> 00:57:58.150
this is highly nonlinear.

00:57:58.150 --> 00:57:59.980
I mean, here's an
example of this.

00:57:59.980 --> 00:58:06.490
x1 to the 1.3 times x2 to
the minus 0.3 is equal to 1.

00:58:06.490 --> 00:58:09.857
Well, that's not-- that is not
affine last time I checked.

00:58:09.857 --> 00:58:10.690
It's not even close.

00:58:10.690 --> 00:58:11.510
Everybody got that?

00:58:11.510 --> 00:58:12.010
OK.

00:58:12.010 --> 00:58:13.510
That's a GP.

00:58:13.510 --> 00:58:16.270
And books were written
about this in the '60s.

00:58:16.270 --> 00:58:19.150
People solved them
by hand, I'm not

00:58:19.150 --> 00:58:21.760
kidding, when they
were easy enough.

00:58:21.760 --> 00:58:24.820
It's got kind of
weird, but then here's

00:58:24.820 --> 00:58:26.770
what was discovered in
the West in the '80s,

00:58:26.770 --> 00:58:28.690
and supposedly in
Moscow, everybody

00:58:28.690 --> 00:58:29.740
knew this in the '60s.

00:58:29.740 --> 00:58:32.550
So here it is.

00:58:32.550 --> 00:58:35.520
What you do is you
change variables.

00:58:35.520 --> 00:58:37.180
They're positive variables.

00:58:37.180 --> 00:58:40.230
So I'm going to work with
the logs, I call those y.

00:58:40.230 --> 00:58:41.770
That's a change of variables.

00:58:41.770 --> 00:58:44.610
So I'm going to have yi.

00:58:44.610 --> 00:58:46.470
And then when I have a monomial.

00:58:46.470 --> 00:58:47.880
What does that convert to?

00:58:47.880 --> 00:58:49.830
Well, it's the log--

00:58:49.830 --> 00:58:53.658
oh, sorry, yeah,
let's see what it is.

00:58:53.658 --> 00:58:57.840
If you have f, this
transforms to-- these

00:58:57.840 --> 00:59:00.270
are exponentials actually
to the exponential

00:59:00.270 --> 00:59:02.010
of an affine function.

00:59:02.010 --> 00:59:05.655
If I take the log of a monomial,
I get an affine function.

00:59:08.400 --> 00:59:11.580
Then a posynomial is
going to transform

00:59:11.580 --> 00:59:15.870
into a log sum exp of an affine
function, and they're convex.

00:59:15.870 --> 00:59:19.830
So that means the geometric
program over here, which is not

00:59:19.830 --> 00:59:24.180
remotely convex,
is actually exactly

00:59:24.180 --> 00:59:26.670
equivalent to this problem,
and this problem is convex,

00:59:26.670 --> 00:59:29.106
because look at this.

00:59:29.106 --> 00:59:32.950
This is log sum x of
an affine function.

00:59:32.950 --> 00:59:37.850
Log sum x is convex, and
convex of affine is convex.

00:59:37.850 --> 00:59:39.730
So are all these.

00:59:39.730 --> 00:59:41.350
That's linear.

00:59:41.350 --> 00:59:41.920
Affine.

00:59:41.920 --> 00:59:43.900
Whatever-- however
you want to say it.

00:59:43.900 --> 00:59:44.920
Everybody got this?

00:59:44.920 --> 00:59:46.210
So it's actually pretty cool.

00:59:46.210 --> 00:59:48.033
It's even cooler
because every time

00:59:48.033 --> 00:59:49.450
when you look at
a practical field

00:59:49.450 --> 00:59:51.370
where people use
geometric programming,

00:59:51.370 --> 00:59:55.810
you will find out that they
already, either implicitly

00:59:55.810 --> 00:59:59.248
or not, figured out you
should be working with logs.

00:59:59.248 --> 01:00:00.290
I'll give you an example.

01:00:00.290 --> 01:00:03.370
A lot of times there's power
control for wireless systems--

01:00:03.370 --> 01:00:06.010
or actually, for cables.

01:00:06.010 --> 01:00:08.800
And actually-- so
engineers there, they

01:00:08.800 --> 01:00:11.710
use decibels to
talk about powers.

01:00:11.710 --> 01:00:12.760
Well, guess what?

01:00:12.760 --> 01:00:14.890
That's the log.

01:00:14.890 --> 01:00:17.198
If you go to circuit
design and you'd say,

01:00:17.198 --> 01:00:18.490
well, tell me about my library.

01:00:18.490 --> 01:00:19.900
What sizes do these come in?

01:00:19.900 --> 01:00:23.140
And they go, oh yeah,
you can get that--

01:00:23.140 --> 01:00:24.910
whatever, that NAND
gate, you can get it

01:00:24.910 --> 01:00:33.630
in a size 0.5, size
1, 1.4, 2, 4, 8, 16.

01:00:33.630 --> 01:00:35.507
Sound familiar?

01:00:35.507 --> 01:00:37.590
What it means is they
already knew in their hearts

01:00:37.590 --> 01:00:40.500
that basically, what was--

01:00:40.500 --> 01:00:42.420
it wasn't-- you should
not work with the size,

01:00:42.420 --> 01:00:44.400
you should work with the
log of the size right

01:00:44.400 --> 01:00:45.983
because that's what
those numbers are.

01:00:45.983 --> 01:00:47.010
So yeah?

01:00:47.010 --> 01:00:50.670
Is it always the same
K in like the objective

01:00:50.670 --> 01:00:53.710
and the inequality?

01:00:53.710 --> 01:00:55.830
Let me think.

01:00:55.830 --> 01:00:59.860
It-- let me think about that.

01:00:59.860 --> 01:01:02.260
Sorry.

01:01:02.260 --> 01:01:03.010
It is.

01:01:03.010 --> 01:01:05.080
Let me-- it may
not be, actually.

01:01:05.080 --> 01:01:05.680
Let me think.

01:01:05.680 --> 01:01:07.097
I don't think it
is, you're right,

01:01:07.097 --> 01:01:09.640
because these are different--

01:01:09.640 --> 01:01:12.550
yeah, there's something-- the
K doesn't have to be the same.

01:01:12.550 --> 01:01:16.490
[INAUDIBLE] because it's--

01:01:16.490 --> 01:01:17.660
No, wait a minute.

01:01:17.660 --> 01:01:19.250
No, it does not have to be.

01:01:19.250 --> 01:01:20.493
So, yeah.

01:01:20.493 --> 01:01:21.410
It doesn't have to be.

01:01:21.410 --> 01:01:23.150
But it's not part of
the definition of--

01:01:23.150 --> 01:01:24.020
No.

01:01:24.020 --> 01:01:25.340
It wouldn't matter.

01:01:25.340 --> 01:01:26.360
So, OK.

01:01:26.360 --> 01:01:29.050
So that's GP.

01:01:29.050 --> 01:01:30.050
I'll go in one example--

01:01:30.050 --> 01:01:31.730
I'm not going to go
through the details.

01:01:31.730 --> 01:01:33.897
I mean, actually, the real
truth is, I couldn't even

01:01:33.897 --> 01:01:36.320
defend the details,
but I will tell you

01:01:36.320 --> 01:01:39.210
a funny story about it.

01:01:39.210 --> 01:01:40.970
So this is on a--

01:01:40.970 --> 01:01:43.880
I found this in a
book on optimization

01:01:43.880 --> 01:01:45.560
for engineering design.

01:01:45.560 --> 01:01:49.970
And this example was
held up as an example--

01:01:49.970 --> 01:01:53.730
they said, oh, here's a
problem that is not convex.

01:01:53.730 --> 01:01:56.800
And that was literally
the point of the example.

01:01:56.800 --> 01:01:58.480
That's a true statement.

01:01:58.480 --> 01:02:02.080
But you do the right
sneaky change of variables

01:02:02.080 --> 01:02:04.930
and it is convex.

01:02:04.930 --> 01:02:07.480
And so the problem
is this, is you're

01:02:07.480 --> 01:02:10.210
going to design this cantilever
beam, and you're very--

01:02:10.210 --> 01:02:14.560
what you can do is you can
change the length-- that's

01:02:14.560 --> 01:02:16.030
this thing that
you could see here,

01:02:16.030 --> 01:02:17.697
but also the width,
which you can't see.

01:02:17.697 --> 01:02:21.560
That's how wide it is over here.

01:02:21.560 --> 01:02:27.370
And so that's what this is.

01:02:27.370 --> 01:02:30.178
And we're going to put
a force at the tip.

01:02:30.178 --> 01:02:31.720
And of course, I
mean, you don't have

01:02:31.720 --> 01:02:33.512
to know any mechanical
engineering to know,

01:02:33.512 --> 01:02:35.380
it's going to deflect.

01:02:35.380 --> 01:02:39.410
And if you make yourself a
giant, thick thing with-- this

01:02:39.410 --> 01:02:41.080
is steel or something
like that, it's

01:02:41.080 --> 01:02:44.380
going to be very stiff
because then when you push,

01:02:44.380 --> 01:02:47.290
you put a lot of newtons on it
and it's barely going to budge.

01:02:47.290 --> 01:02:50.080
Or I could make it
this flimsy small thing

01:02:50.080 --> 01:02:52.240
and I press it and it's
going to really bend.

01:02:52.240 --> 01:02:54.850
So that's-- I mean-- and again,
you don't have to know any

01:02:54.850 --> 01:02:56.350
mechanical engineering
to know this.

01:02:56.350 --> 01:02:58.400
I mean, I don't know
any, so that's fine.

01:02:58.400 --> 01:03:02.840
So it's fine, but
that's the rough idea.

01:03:02.840 --> 01:03:06.200
And so your design problem
is something like, minimize

01:03:06.200 --> 01:03:08.810
the total weight subject to--

01:03:08.810 --> 01:03:10.040
I mean, these are just--

01:03:10.040 --> 01:03:12.410
these could even be
manufacturing constraints

01:03:12.410 --> 01:03:16.340
that you just-- that's
just limits to these.

01:03:16.340 --> 01:03:19.580
You can have an upper that's
a geometric constraint.

01:03:19.580 --> 01:03:22.340
You can have an upper bound
on the stress in each segment

01:03:22.340 --> 01:03:26.120
that that's a reasonable
model of failure.

01:03:26.120 --> 01:03:28.460
And then you want an upper
bound on the deflection

01:03:28.460 --> 01:03:29.780
of the beam at the end.

01:03:29.780 --> 01:03:32.690
So what you're saying is if I
load this with 10 kilonewtons,

01:03:32.690 --> 01:03:36.230
I don't want it I don't want it
to sag more than 3 centimeters.

01:03:36.230 --> 01:03:37.790
That would be your spec.

01:03:37.790 --> 01:03:39.320
Everybody got it?

01:03:39.320 --> 01:03:42.590
And by the way, problems
like this but simpler

01:03:42.590 --> 01:03:46.460
were already being solved in
the early 1950s in aerospace.

01:03:46.460 --> 01:03:49.310
They were doing exact-- and they
were using linear programming

01:03:49.310 --> 01:03:49.820
and--

01:03:49.820 --> 01:03:52.190
mostly linear
programming in aerospace.

01:03:52.190 --> 01:03:57.020
Pretty cool, because if you
design a bridge that people

01:03:57.020 --> 01:03:58.910
are going to drive
over for 100 years,

01:03:58.910 --> 01:04:01.250
you build a lot of margin
into it and stuff like that.

01:04:01.250 --> 01:04:04.490
If you're doing aerospace
stuff, you can't--

01:04:04.490 --> 01:04:06.905
you can't engineer stuff
5x over because it just

01:04:06.905 --> 01:04:08.780
weighs too much, and
congratulations, now you

01:04:08.780 --> 01:04:10.430
have no payload.

01:04:10.430 --> 01:04:14.660
So there, it's delicate.

01:04:14.660 --> 01:04:18.480
You want things to be as
absolutely as light as possible

01:04:18.480 --> 01:04:20.490
with--

01:04:20.490 --> 01:04:24.120
but still actually satisfy
all the requirements that

01:04:24.120 --> 01:04:26.460
it's actually going to
work, which means that it

01:04:26.460 --> 01:04:28.290
can't-- it's gotta
be stiff enough,

01:04:28.290 --> 01:04:30.210
it's got to handle all
sorts of dynamic loads,

01:04:30.210 --> 01:04:31.168
all that kind of stuff.

01:04:31.168 --> 01:04:32.580
So anyway, so that's the idea.

01:04:32.580 --> 01:04:34.528
So stuff like this
is done all the time.

01:04:34.528 --> 01:04:35.820
In fact, there's a whole field.

01:04:35.820 --> 01:04:36.420
It doesn't matter.

01:04:36.420 --> 01:04:37.545
I'll talk about that later.

01:04:37.545 --> 01:04:38.470
OK, so that's it.

01:04:38.470 --> 01:04:38.970
OK.

01:04:38.970 --> 01:04:41.040
So here it is in English.

01:04:41.040 --> 01:04:43.840
And then in words to work
out what these things are,

01:04:43.840 --> 01:04:44.340
I'll just--

01:04:44.340 --> 01:04:45.630
I'm going to just
talk about a few

01:04:45.630 --> 01:04:46.620
of the things and then not--

01:04:46.620 --> 01:04:48.453
I don't want to focus
on it because it's not

01:04:48.453 --> 01:04:49.630
that critical to us.

01:04:49.630 --> 01:04:51.420
But here's the total weight.

01:04:51.420 --> 01:04:53.550
The variables,
remember, are w and h.

01:04:53.550 --> 01:04:56.503
And I would like to ask you,
what kind of function is that?

01:04:56.503 --> 01:04:58.920
I mean, aside from it being a
posynomial, which you should

01:04:58.920 --> 01:05:02.430
not never say in public,
but what kind of function

01:05:02.430 --> 01:05:04.650
is this of w and h?

01:05:04.650 --> 01:05:06.630
Again, pretend you're in public.

01:05:06.630 --> 01:05:09.630
So you can use words that
you're allowed to say in public.

01:05:09.630 --> 01:05:12.010
What kind of function is that?

01:05:12.010 --> 01:05:12.920
Scalar problem?

01:05:12.920 --> 01:05:13.580
It's what?

01:05:13.580 --> 01:05:15.650
A scalar problem?

01:05:15.650 --> 01:05:16.430
I missed that.

01:05:16.430 --> 01:05:17.270
Dot product?

01:05:17.270 --> 01:05:18.260
It's a dot product.

01:05:18.260 --> 01:05:19.640
Right.

01:05:19.640 --> 01:05:22.370
It's an inner product
between w and h.

01:05:22.370 --> 01:05:25.850
Is it convex in w and h?

01:05:25.850 --> 01:05:26.420
No.

01:05:26.420 --> 01:05:28.220
It's in quadratic form.

01:05:28.220 --> 01:05:33.650
It's the quadratic form
with matrix 0, like i, i0.

01:05:33.650 --> 01:05:35.150
So that is not--

01:05:35.150 --> 01:05:36.830
this is not convex.

01:05:36.830 --> 01:05:40.040
So you know already, we are
not solving a convex problem.

01:05:40.040 --> 01:05:40.762
Or-- yeah.

01:05:40.762 --> 01:05:42.470
Well, when we get
around to solving this,

01:05:42.470 --> 01:05:43.520
we'll do this
change of variables

01:05:43.520 --> 01:05:45.140
and it will become
a convex problem.

01:05:45.140 --> 01:05:47.540
OK.

01:05:47.540 --> 01:05:48.040
OK.

01:05:48.040 --> 01:05:50.860
So these various
ratios are monomials,

01:05:50.860 --> 01:05:53.470
and the maximum
stress is a monomial,

01:05:53.470 --> 01:05:55.360
so those are fine too to handle.

01:05:55.360 --> 01:05:58.420
The vertical deflection
comes from-- again,

01:05:58.420 --> 01:06:00.850
I couldn't defend
this, but this is--

01:06:00.850 --> 01:06:03.400
I mean, I suppose it's
just like elementary baby

01:06:03.400 --> 01:06:04.840
physics and mechanics
or whatever,

01:06:04.840 --> 01:06:08.080
but it's just-- you start
at the tip and you go back

01:06:08.080 --> 01:06:10.120
and this just a recursion
that tells you this.

01:06:10.120 --> 01:06:16.400
What's kind of cool about this
is look at various things here.

01:06:16.400 --> 01:06:21.160
But what you'll see is that
each of these, if you start--

01:06:21.160 --> 01:06:22.930
these-- you can
actually, by applying

01:06:22.930 --> 01:06:25.690
the rules-- oh, I should say
there's a full other set.

01:06:25.690 --> 01:06:30.550
You can transcribe all the rules
you know for convex functions,

01:06:30.550 --> 01:06:35.620
and they will have analogs for
like posynomials and monomials.

01:06:35.620 --> 01:06:37.390
And I'll give you some example.

01:06:37.390 --> 01:06:38.830
Let's just go back.

01:06:38.830 --> 01:06:43.370
I mean, OK.

01:06:43.370 --> 01:06:44.300
OK, here we go.

01:06:44.300 --> 01:06:46.160
I want to do this.

01:06:46.160 --> 01:06:49.040
Talk to me about the
product of monomials.

01:06:49.040 --> 01:06:50.300
What is that?

01:06:50.300 --> 01:06:52.836
Product of two monomials.

01:06:52.836 --> 01:06:55.160
[INAUDIBLE]

01:06:55.160 --> 01:06:55.660
No, sir.

01:06:55.660 --> 01:06:58.460
Two-- I have to
monomial functions.

01:06:58.460 --> 01:07:00.877
Again, not in the math sense,
in the geometric programming

01:07:00.877 --> 01:07:01.377
sense.

01:07:01.377 --> 01:07:03.650
I have two monomial functions
and I take the product.

01:07:03.650 --> 01:07:05.090
What is it?

01:07:05.090 --> 01:07:06.050
It's monomial.

01:07:06.050 --> 01:07:07.340
A monomial.

01:07:07.340 --> 01:07:10.250
What's the sum of two monomials?

01:07:10.250 --> 01:07:11.210
[INAUDIBLE]

01:07:11.210 --> 01:07:11.790
[INAUDIBLE]

01:07:11.790 --> 01:07:12.290
Right.

01:07:12.290 --> 01:07:12.790
OK.

01:07:12.790 --> 01:07:15.938
What's the product
of two posynomials?

01:07:15.938 --> 01:07:17.270
[INAUDIBLE]

01:07:17.270 --> 01:07:17.810
All right.

01:07:17.810 --> 01:07:22.070
What's a-- what is a polynomial
divided by a monomial?

01:07:25.148 --> 01:07:26.460
[INAUDIBLE]

01:07:26.460 --> 01:07:28.240
It's a posynomial.

01:07:28.240 --> 01:07:29.650
[INAUDIBLE]

01:07:29.650 --> 01:07:31.870
What's a positive
divided by a posynomial?

01:07:37.380 --> 01:07:37.890
Right.

01:07:37.890 --> 01:07:40.170
The answer is nothing.

01:07:40.170 --> 01:07:41.248
We don't know.

01:07:41.248 --> 01:07:43.290
So I'm just saying there's
a bunch of these rules

01:07:43.290 --> 01:07:46.253
that you're going to use.

01:07:46.253 --> 01:07:48.420
And down here-- I don't
want to go into the details,

01:07:48.420 --> 01:07:52.397
but you just argue that a sum
of polynomials is a polynomial

01:07:52.397 --> 01:07:53.730
or whatever, that kind of thing.

01:07:53.730 --> 01:07:57.030
And what you'll end up finding
is that these things are just

01:07:57.030 --> 01:07:58.050
postnomial functions.

01:07:58.050 --> 01:08:01.590
They're quite complicated, but
they are polynomial functions.

01:08:01.590 --> 01:08:05.880
Anyway, then you reassemble
everything to look like this.

01:08:05.880 --> 01:08:11.790
And there it is in
this standard GP form.

01:08:11.790 --> 01:08:16.950
By the way, you
don't have to do this

01:08:16.950 --> 01:08:20.370
because what's clear is that
you can actually write these out

01:08:20.370 --> 01:08:23.310
in some object-oriented-- in
some domain-specific language,

01:08:23.310 --> 01:08:26.670
and then something would do
all of this stuff for you.

01:08:26.670 --> 01:08:28.170
And the truth is,
you shouldn't even

01:08:28.170 --> 01:08:31.200
look at this any more than you
should look-- if you actually--

01:08:31.200 --> 01:08:34.020
the way you should look at like
after you compile something,

01:08:34.020 --> 01:08:35.670
why would you look
at the bytecode

01:08:35.670 --> 01:08:36.870
or something like that?

01:08:36.870 --> 01:08:38.649
I mean, you could.

01:08:38.649 --> 01:08:40.240
A very small number
of people have

01:08:40.240 --> 01:08:42.490
to because that's what
they do, but the rest of us

01:08:42.490 --> 01:08:44.870
should never look at that,
you know what I'm saying?

01:08:44.870 --> 01:08:47.770
And I think the same is
kind of true for this.

01:08:47.770 --> 01:08:50.930
So, OK.

01:08:50.930 --> 01:08:53.520
I think I'll skip this one.

01:08:53.520 --> 01:08:55.520
This is just some
advanced topics.

01:08:55.520 --> 01:08:59.420
And I'll talk about
a couple more things.

01:08:59.420 --> 01:09:02.330
Just-- actually, two
more main topics.

01:09:02.330 --> 01:09:03.960
OK.

01:09:03.960 --> 01:09:07.529
Oh, to finish up the other
one, geometric programming

01:09:07.529 --> 01:09:13.590
is your first example of a wide
class of non-convex problems

01:09:13.590 --> 01:09:17.729
that can be transformed to
equivalent convex problems,

01:09:17.729 --> 01:09:19.200
and therefore, solved.

01:09:19.200 --> 01:09:21.810
So that's the main
point about it.

01:09:21.810 --> 01:09:25.710
So it's actually kind of-- we're
going to see plenty of others.

01:09:25.710 --> 01:09:28.170
We'll see it also
happening in statistics,

01:09:28.170 --> 01:09:31.470
and it happens in control
in other areas where--

01:09:31.470 --> 01:09:33.069
in fact, I'll even
tell you a story.

01:09:33.069 --> 01:09:34.569
I used to go around
and give talks--

01:09:34.569 --> 01:09:36.240
this is a long, long time ago.

01:09:36.240 --> 01:09:38.370
And I would say, look,
here's a problem in control.

01:09:38.370 --> 01:09:39.779
It's convex, here's
another problem in control.

01:09:39.779 --> 01:09:40.830
Oh, look, it's convex.

01:09:40.830 --> 01:09:41.670
And I said--

01:09:41.670 --> 01:09:43.319
I had a slide that was like--

01:09:43.319 --> 01:09:45.899
I said, here's an
example of a problem.

01:09:45.899 --> 01:09:49.050
And I said, I don't
think it's convex.

01:09:49.050 --> 01:09:49.752
So anyway.

01:09:49.752 --> 01:09:51.210
So I went around
and gave that talk

01:09:51.210 --> 01:09:52.979
to several thousand people.

01:09:52.979 --> 01:09:53.729
I was at Berkeley.

01:09:53.729 --> 01:09:55.290
I gave the talk.

01:09:55.290 --> 01:09:57.120
Colleague of mine came
up to me afterwards

01:09:57.120 --> 01:10:00.120
and he said, oh, you see that
problem that you just said

01:10:00.120 --> 01:10:01.350
was probably non-convex?

01:10:01.350 --> 01:10:02.357
I was like, yeah.

01:10:02.357 --> 01:10:03.940
And he said, can I
show you something?

01:10:03.940 --> 01:10:05.260
I was like, sure.

01:10:05.260 --> 01:10:10.030
Anyway, he had some completely
sick, demented change

01:10:10.030 --> 01:10:10.690
of variables.

01:10:10.690 --> 01:10:12.700
Like instead of this
positive definite matrix,

01:10:12.700 --> 01:10:13.690
we're going to work
in the inverse.

01:10:13.690 --> 01:10:14.380
And instead of
these, we're going

01:10:14.380 --> 01:10:16.390
to work in the inverse
of this times that.

01:10:16.390 --> 01:10:20.170
And I was like, Andy,
why would anyone do that?

01:10:20.170 --> 01:10:21.910
And he goes, watch this.

01:10:21.910 --> 01:10:25.360
Two lines later, it's convex,
and I step away and I'm like,

01:10:25.360 --> 01:10:25.940
oh no!

01:10:25.940 --> 01:10:26.440
Oh no!

01:10:26.440 --> 01:10:28.090
I've been telling people
all over the world

01:10:28.090 --> 01:10:29.298
that it was not going to be--

01:10:29.298 --> 01:10:31.870
Then we looked at my slide
and it was really awesome.

01:10:31.870 --> 01:10:34.100
It says-- it says a
problem that's not convex.

01:10:34.100 --> 01:10:36.740
And there was a little footnote,
and it said "probably."

01:10:36.740 --> 01:10:37.930
[LAUGHTER]

01:10:37.930 --> 01:10:38.950
So I was safe.

01:10:38.950 --> 01:10:41.590
Then, by the way, after that,
I told that story every time.

01:10:41.590 --> 01:10:43.420
So I said-- so, yeah.

01:10:43.420 --> 01:10:47.090
So basically you should be
very careful because this--

01:10:47.090 --> 01:10:47.590
anyway.

01:10:47.590 --> 01:10:49.100
So, OK.

01:10:49.100 --> 01:10:49.600
Fine.

01:10:49.600 --> 01:10:50.560
Back to this one.

01:10:50.560 --> 01:10:52.480
Now we're back to
honest convex problems.

01:10:52.480 --> 01:10:55.240
OK, so, OK.

01:10:55.240 --> 01:10:57.850
So all we do here
is the constraints,

01:10:57.850 --> 01:11:00.760
we're going to have
vector constraints instead

01:11:00.760 --> 01:11:04.470
of just scalar inequalities.

01:11:04.470 --> 01:11:09.060
A very famous example of
that is called a conic form

01:11:09.060 --> 01:11:11.648
problem or a cone form problem.

01:11:11.648 --> 01:11:12.690
And that looks like this.

01:11:12.690 --> 01:11:15.750
You minimize a linear
function subject to Fx plus g

01:11:15.750 --> 01:11:17.100
is less than or equal to 0.

01:11:17.100 --> 01:11:20.250
But that's with respect to
some cone K and Ax equals b.

01:11:20.250 --> 01:11:23.940
What's cool is, if K is
the non-negative orthant,

01:11:23.940 --> 01:11:27.670
this is just a pedantic
way to write down an LP.

01:11:27.670 --> 01:11:28.170
but.

01:11:28.170 --> 01:11:31.110
The cool part is, K
could be other cones,

01:11:31.110 --> 01:11:33.330
and then you get--
like if I make

01:11:33.330 --> 01:11:37.260
K a product of second-order
cones, this is SOCP.

01:11:37.260 --> 01:11:39.040
But I can have
other things, too,

01:11:39.040 --> 01:11:42.090
and a very famous one is
so-called semidefinite

01:11:42.090 --> 01:11:42.910
programming.

01:11:42.910 --> 01:11:44.880
So that's something
you will see.

01:11:44.880 --> 01:11:47.980
This is probably-- it's
not bad to have heard it.

01:11:47.980 --> 01:11:51.480
People talk about it in actually
a whole bunch of fields now.

01:11:51.480 --> 01:11:53.220
So in theoretical
computer science,

01:11:53.220 --> 01:11:54.780
people blabber about it.

01:11:54.780 --> 01:11:56.730
People in physics use it.

01:11:56.730 --> 01:12:01.260
So it's become mainstream
in the last 20, 25 years.

01:12:01.260 --> 01:12:02.160
Here it is.

01:12:02.160 --> 01:12:05.110
You minimize a linear function,
subject to equality constraint.

01:12:05.110 --> 01:12:06.910
Linear equality,
it looks-- and then

01:12:06.910 --> 01:12:10.370
you have a single inequality
which looks like this.

01:12:10.370 --> 01:12:12.310
In fact, it looks like
a linear inequality.

01:12:12.310 --> 01:12:15.688
If these were scalars and
that was ordinary less than

01:12:15.688 --> 01:12:17.980
or equal to, this would be
a single linear inequality--

01:12:17.980 --> 01:12:18.940
it'd be a silly LP.

01:12:18.940 --> 01:12:22.330
But here, these are
symmetric matrices,

01:12:22.330 --> 01:12:24.400
and this says this
linear combination--

01:12:24.400 --> 01:12:28.720
this affine function, which
is a symmetric matrix,

01:12:28.720 --> 01:12:31.960
has to be negative semidefinite.

01:12:31.960 --> 01:12:36.220
So that's called a
semidefinite program.

01:12:36.220 --> 01:12:38.830
And-- oh, this thing
is called an LMI,

01:12:38.830 --> 01:12:40.212
a Linear Matrix Inequality.

01:12:40.212 --> 01:12:41.920
It's like a linear
inequality except it's

01:12:41.920 --> 01:12:42.980
a matrix inequality.

01:12:42.980 --> 01:12:44.560
So, OK.

01:12:44.560 --> 01:12:47.500
And there's things that you do.

01:12:47.500 --> 01:12:50.500
This says you only have one, but
you could just diagonally stack

01:12:50.500 --> 01:12:53.230
them like this.

01:12:53.230 --> 01:12:53.850
Yeah.

01:12:53.850 --> 01:12:55.450
Oh, any suggestion?

01:12:55.450 --> 01:13:02.880
How would I make-- how would
I represent an LP as an SDP?

01:13:02.880 --> 01:13:04.290
Any idea?

01:13:04.290 --> 01:13:05.970
You have multiple--

01:13:05.970 --> 01:13:09.390
I have 10 linear
inequalities on x scalar.

01:13:09.390 --> 01:13:10.710
How do I-- yeah?

01:13:10.710 --> 01:13:12.550
--a diagonal matrix?

01:13:12.550 --> 01:13:13.050
Yeah.

01:13:13.050 --> 01:13:16.830
You make these matrices all
diagonal, and that's diagonal.

01:13:16.830 --> 01:13:19.980
And then it's silly
because a diagonal matrix

01:13:19.980 --> 01:13:22.170
is less than or equal to 0.

01:13:22.170 --> 01:13:24.690
I mean, if and only if
all the entries are.

01:13:24.690 --> 01:13:28.440
So this extends
LP, you would say.

01:13:28.440 --> 01:13:31.920
So, OK, this is a
semidefinite program.

01:13:31.920 --> 01:13:32.940
OK.

01:13:32.940 --> 01:13:36.340
And, as I said, you can
easily represent these things.

01:13:36.340 --> 01:13:38.220
It also extends SDP.

01:13:38.220 --> 01:13:39.750
And-- sorry, SOCP.

01:13:39.750 --> 01:13:43.890
And that's also interesting,
not obvious at all.

01:13:43.890 --> 01:13:46.020
And in fact, that goes
through something called

01:13:46.020 --> 01:13:50.250
the Schur complement, which--

01:13:50.250 --> 01:13:54.420
she put that on one of
the next coming homeworks.

01:13:54.420 --> 01:13:54.930
Yeah.

01:13:54.930 --> 01:13:55.440
OK, sorry.

01:13:55.440 --> 01:13:55.740
OK.

01:13:55.740 --> 01:13:56.240
OK.

01:13:56.240 --> 01:14:00.522
So Schur compliment, it's this.

01:14:00.522 --> 01:14:04.680
This says-- it has to
do with a block matrix.

01:14:04.680 --> 01:14:05.850
By the way, this is not--

01:14:05.850 --> 01:14:07.517
everyone here has
seen Schur complement.

01:14:07.517 --> 01:14:10.620
It's just people
didn't use that name.

01:14:10.620 --> 01:14:12.600
So if you're in
statistics or probability,

01:14:12.600 --> 01:14:15.690
I mean, either you are or you
took those classes, I hope,

01:14:15.690 --> 01:14:17.880
then this is conditioning
on a Gaussian.

01:14:17.880 --> 01:14:19.525
But it's tons of other stuff.

01:14:19.525 --> 01:14:21.150
In mechanical--
electrical engineering,

01:14:21.150 --> 01:14:23.130
it means you take an
electrical circuit

01:14:23.130 --> 01:14:25.230
and you terminate a
bunch of the ports.

01:14:25.230 --> 01:14:28.260
Like you short those and
you open-circuit those,

01:14:28.260 --> 01:14:30.750
and then you ask, what does
the rest of it look like?

01:14:30.750 --> 01:14:33.210
This is all-- all these
things are Schur compliments.

01:14:33.210 --> 01:14:34.780
So here it is.

01:14:34.780 --> 01:14:36.600
It says that a matrix--

01:14:36.600 --> 01:14:38.610
I mean, I'm not going
to go into it now,

01:14:38.610 --> 01:14:42.060
but this inequality, which
notices like nonlinear--

01:14:42.060 --> 01:14:45.690
it's got this 2-norm, is
equivalent to this thing.

01:14:45.690 --> 01:14:48.540
And this is-- that's a
matrix, which is affine in x.

01:14:48.540 --> 01:14:51.520
And the argument is,
this thing is positive

01:14:51.520 --> 01:14:53.470
semi-definite if
and only if that's

01:14:53.470 --> 01:14:54.790
bigger than or equal to 0.

01:14:54.790 --> 01:14:58.060
And this thing minus that
times the inverse of that times

01:14:58.060 --> 01:15:05.020
that is going to be less
than or equal to a 0.

01:15:05.020 --> 01:15:07.540
So that-- or bigger
than or equal to 0 here,

01:15:07.540 --> 01:15:09.520
so that's-- so I won't
go into that detail,

01:15:09.520 --> 01:15:12.700
but it's interesting.

01:15:12.700 --> 01:15:15.140
So, I'll do an example.

01:15:15.140 --> 01:15:17.943
Here's an example.

01:15:17.943 --> 01:15:19.360
And these are--
like we're already

01:15:19.360 --> 01:15:22.180
in the domain of like these
are not obvious problems.

01:15:22.180 --> 01:15:23.380
So here's one.

01:15:23.380 --> 01:15:26.230
I have a bunch of
symmetric matrices.

01:15:26.230 --> 01:15:29.590
I'm going to form an
affine combination of them.

01:15:29.590 --> 01:15:32.620
No, I mean I'm going to form--
this is an affine function

01:15:32.620 --> 01:15:34.550
with these coefficients.

01:15:34.550 --> 01:15:36.940
And I'd like you
to choose x1 to xn

01:15:36.940 --> 01:15:40.030
to minimize the maximum
eigenvalue of that matrix.

01:15:40.030 --> 01:15:41.365
Now we already discussed this.

01:15:41.365 --> 01:15:43.690
You know that's convex.

01:15:43.690 --> 01:15:45.640
But that doesn't
mean it's obvious

01:15:45.640 --> 01:15:46.780
how to solve that problem.

01:15:46.780 --> 01:15:49.030
It turns out, it's
just an SDP, and it

01:15:49.030 --> 01:15:50.770
just-- it literally
looks like that.

01:15:50.770 --> 01:15:51.830
It's really dumb.

01:15:51.830 --> 01:15:54.580
This is-- by the way,
that's an epigraph

01:15:54.580 --> 01:15:57.220
for the maximum eigenvalue.

01:15:57.220 --> 01:15:59.500
That the maximum eigenvalue
is less than something

01:15:59.500 --> 01:16:02.080
if and only if that matrix is
less than or equal to-- times

01:16:02.080 --> 01:16:05.390
t to the identity.

01:16:05.390 --> 01:16:05.890
OK.

01:16:05.890 --> 01:16:07.455
So that's the idea.

01:16:07.455 --> 01:16:08.830
But-- by the way,
how many people

01:16:08.830 --> 01:16:13.510
here have encountered or
heard of SDP somewhere else?

01:16:13.510 --> 01:16:14.470
Just for fun.

01:16:14.470 --> 01:16:15.020
You did?

01:16:15.020 --> 01:16:15.520
Cool.

01:16:15.520 --> 01:16:16.020
Where?

01:16:16.020 --> 01:16:16.870
What context?

01:16:16.870 --> 01:16:19.240
I did theoretical
computer science.

01:16:19.240 --> 01:16:20.210
Oh, there we go.

01:16:20.210 --> 01:16:20.710
Awesome.

01:16:20.710 --> 01:16:21.777
Right.

01:16:21.777 --> 01:16:23.860
Did anyone know that you
could actually solve them

01:16:23.860 --> 01:16:25.960
except for theoretically?

01:16:25.960 --> 01:16:26.800
No, not really.

01:16:26.800 --> 01:16:27.860
OK, yeah, I thought so.

01:16:27.860 --> 01:16:27.940
OK.

01:16:27.940 --> 01:16:28.270
That's fine.

01:16:28.270 --> 01:16:28.770
That's cool.

01:16:28.770 --> 01:16:29.860
Totally cool.

01:16:29.860 --> 01:16:31.390
I have lots of
friends who do that.

01:16:31.390 --> 01:16:32.500
There was another one.

01:16:32.500 --> 01:16:34.420
[INAUDIBLE]

01:16:34.420 --> 01:16:35.390
Yeah, stats.

01:16:35.390 --> 01:16:35.890
What?

01:16:35.890 --> 01:16:37.090
In statistics.

01:16:37.090 --> 01:16:38.020
In statistics.

01:16:38.020 --> 01:16:39.160
OK, cool, Yeah.

01:16:39.160 --> 01:16:39.670
OK, good.

01:16:39.670 --> 01:16:41.740
So it's getting-- I
mean, it's obviously not

01:16:41.740 --> 01:16:43.930
totally mainstream or
half the class would

01:16:43.930 --> 01:16:47.270
have raised their hand,
but it's getting there.

01:16:47.270 --> 01:16:51.670
It's not like-- it's
not crazy stuff anymore.

01:16:51.670 --> 01:16:53.200
OK.

01:16:53.200 --> 01:16:54.700
Well, here's an interesting one.

01:16:54.700 --> 01:16:59.222
Minimize-- this is a 2-norm.

01:16:59.222 --> 01:17:00.430
So that's the induced 2-norm.

01:17:00.430 --> 01:17:04.150
It's the maximum singular
value of a matrix.

01:17:04.150 --> 01:17:07.630
And it says, please minimize
that over some affine set.

01:17:07.630 --> 01:17:10.390
And this, too, you
do the same thing.

01:17:10.390 --> 01:17:17.110
You express this as an LMI,
and then everything works out.

01:17:17.110 --> 01:17:20.050
And the conclusion is we can
just solve these problems now.

01:17:20.050 --> 01:17:21.860
We just we can just solve them.

01:17:21.860 --> 01:17:25.540
So it's actually-- it's
interesting and cool.

01:17:25.540 --> 01:17:26.200
Yeah.

01:17:26.200 --> 01:17:27.670
So, OK.

01:17:27.670 --> 01:17:30.130
And I think what we'll do
